INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4777,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3951028','Highly reliable procedural teams: the journey to spread the universal protocol in diagnostic imaging.','Ross J, Wolf D, Reece K.','2014','whiteboards glitch climates inhospital pronged wla learnings distractions wla wla hrpt hrst wla vocalizing kickoff psychometrically climates wla deliverables overseen hrpt kicked hrpt distractions deliverables solidifying glitch glitches whiteboards kickoff hrpt wla transcending curse hardwire debriefings glitch hrpt hrpt entree kickoff reengaged whiteboards tightened hrpt fredrick debra cphq cphrm learnings louden els louden camus journalist oakbrook terrace c2013 jointcommission standards_information nemcek weiser nejmsa0810119 nejmsa0810119 convissar retta mazzocco petitti davoren jradnu fanelli haage hausegger lienden cirse s00270 s00270 bullbulia farris doty bloomington _asset 1hht9h nonor interactive0912 reston pgts us_guided_breast helmreich neilands dearing staley fahey kuruppu s1553 holzmueller timmel holzmueller paine schulick cusp s1553 helmreich makary freischlag beholder harpercollins paterick paterick waterhouse paterick 0b013e3181be2a88 0b013e3181be2a88','The Joint Commission’s Universal Protocol has been widely implemented in surgical settings since publication in 2003, and the elements are applied to procedures occurring in other health care arenas, in particular, diagnostic imaging. The teams underwent human factors training and then adapted key interventions used in surgical suites to their workflows. Perception of the safety climate improved 25% in interventional radiology and 4.5% in mammography. Perception of the teamwork climate decreased 5.4% in interventional radiology and 16.6% in mammography. The study reveals unexpected challenges and requires long-term effort and focus. Abstract Context: The Joint Commission’s Universal Protocol has been widely implemented in surgical settings since publication in 2003. The elements improved patient safety in operating rooms, and the same rigor is being applied to procedures occurring in other health care arenas, in particular, diagnostic imaging. Objective: In 2011, Kaiser Permanente West Los Angeles’s Diagnostic Imaging Department desired to adapt previous work on Universal Protocol implementation to improve patient safety in interventional radiology and mammography procedures. Design: The teams underwent human factors training and then adapted key interventions used in surgical suites to their workflows. Time-out posters, use of whiteboards, “glitch books,” and regular audits provided structure to overcome the risks that human factors present. Main Outcome Measures: Staff and physician perceptions of the teamwork and safety climates in their modalities were measured using the Safety Attitudes Questionnaire at baseline and at 18 months after training. Unusual Occurrence Reports were also reviewed to identify events and near misses that could be prevented. Implementation of key process changes were identified as process measures. Results: Perception of the safety climate improved 25% in interventional radiology and 4.5% in mammography. Perception of the teamwork climate decreased 5.4% in interventional radiology and 16.6% in mammography. Unusual occurrences were underreported at baseline, and there is ongoing reluctance to document near misses. Conclusion: This work provides important considerations of the impact of departmental cultures for the implementation of the Universal Protocol in procedural areas. It also reveals unexpected challenges, and requires long-term effort and focus. Introduction The Joint Commission’s Universal Protocol 1 has been widely implemented in surgical settings across the country since its publication in 2003. Following implementation of the 3 key elements of the protocol—1) pre-procedure verification, 2) site marking, and 3) a time-out—rates of wrong site, wrong procedure, and wrong person surgery were shown to significantly decrease. 2 For example, Haynes et al 3 measured the impact of the implementation at 8 hospitals in 8 cities as part of the World Health Organization’s Safe Surgery Saves Lives program in 2007 and 2008. The total inhospital rate of death dropped from 1.5% to 0.8%, and the rate of complications fell from 11% to 7%. They also reported that “the overall rates of surgical-site infection and unplanned reoperation ... declined significantly.” 3 Institutions promoting patient safety are advocating for implementation beyond the surgical suite. According to a recent article in the Pennsylvania Patient Safety Advisory, “Despite quality improvement efforts, the prevalence of these errors in other disciplines, namely, radiology services, may be more common than generally expected and reported in the literature.” 4 The work described in this article is the first reported work of its kind at Kaiser Permanente (KP), where the Universal Protocol has been applied to procedural areas in a Diagnostic Imaging Department to decrease errors and improve patient safety. A three-pronged approach to improve safety in these settings was proposed: 1) use human factors concepts and training to create highly reliable procedural teams and to reduce procedural errors, 2) identify a core bundle of metrics that measure performance improvement and rigorously support improvement, and 3) implement simulation-based education. 5 The literature in this arena further shows that teams that “exhibited less teamwork behaviors were at a higher risk for death or complications [and therefore] supports arguments in favor of human factors training for surgical teams.” 6 In KP West Los Angeles (WLA), the goal of the Diagnostic Imaging Department’s leadership was to build on the recommendations in the arena of patient safety in the surgical suite and to apply them to diagnostic imaging procedures. This article will provide lessons learned and tools that other Diagnostic Imaging Departments can use, leading to improved reliability of patient safety in their interventions over time. Methods Literature Search Literature searches were conducted in 2010 and 2012 for state-of-the-art patient safety procedures in interventional radiology and mammography. There are emerging publications addressing the transfer of learnings from the surgical arena to interventional radiology. In 2006, Children’s Hospital of Boston published its work about developing a checklist for interventional radiology, taking into account the unique challenges of the diagnostic imaging environment relative to the rapidly changing scope of interventional radiology. 7 ... behaviors that have been shown to create risks to patient safety and clinical outcomes. This included a discussion of avoidable distractions and limits to human memory, as well as unresolved conflict and lack of respect among team members. This early work has been followed by more general recommendations. In 2008, Angle et al 2 proposed guidelines for implementation of the Universal Protocol in interventional radiology, and recommended that “each institution/hospital ... define thresholds as needed for the process steps ... to meet its quality improvement program needs.” In 2011, the Cardiovascular and Interventional Society of Europe created a task force to create a checklist for interventional radiology based on the World Health Organization surgical safety checklist. The intention was to create a template for interventional radiology care across Europe in recognition of the effectiveness of checklists in surgical suites and to document complication rates in interventional radiology. 8 For example, Lewis et al 9 reported a 0.2% complication rate in interventional radiology when vascular surgical intervention was needed. Literature searches revealed a dearth of information on Universal Protocol implementation in mammography. In September 2012, after this work began at KP WLA, the Institute for Clinical Systems Improvement published a Non-Operating Room Procedural Safety protocol. The Institute provided tools and workflows for pre-procedural verification and time-outs, listing breast biopsies as one of the “invasive, high-risk or surgical procedures” to which their work applies. 10 The American College of Radiology references the Universal Protocol and time-outs in their practice guidelines for breast procedures. 11 Participants Leadership at KP WLA initiated this project and titled it “Highly Reliable Procedural Teams” (HRPT), following the model of KP’s previous implementation of “Highly Reliable Surgical Teams” (HRST), which expands on use of the Universal Protocol in KP surgical suites. Interventional radiology and mammography were identified as the targets for the work. Interventional radiology was identified by KP WLA leadership as an area of vulnerability, and the modalities had been identified as such by the American College of Radiology, as cited in Miller et al, 12 and by the previously mentioned Pennsylvania Patient Safety Advisory. 4 Mammography was included in response to staff vocalizing concern with the safety climate and their inability to effect change. A Steering Committee was established with key leaders from across the Medical Center, as well as staff from the department. Invited members included the senior leaders from the service line; Diagnostic Imaging departmental administrative and physician leadership; staff and physician leaders from both modalities; the patient safety officer; the quality physician lead; the Director of Risk Management; the Director of Quality and Accreditation, Regulation, and Licensing; and a project manager. Measures Safety Attitudes Questionnaires were completed by staff and physicians in the mammography and interventional radiology modalities before kickoff and 18 months later. This questionnaire has proved to be a psychometrically sound and highly reliable instrument on the basis of analysis of responses from 10,843 health care workers in 203 clinical settings and 3 countries. 13 The Teamwork and Safety Climate Survey version of the Safety Attitudes Questionnaire was chosen for this project. It is made up of 27 questions organized into 2 sections, the results of which give the team a sense of overall teamwork and safety climates. The second piece of data came from Unusual Occurrence Reports submitted electronically by staff over the two years before the project and throughout the project implementation. The KP WLA Risk Management Department maintains these data. Reports of events, including near misses, were sorted according to modality and type of event and were reported to the Steering Committee. Annual updates are conducted to measure impact. Several process measures were identified as well. First, data on the percentage of staff and physicians that attended human factors training were collected. Participation rates were used to show engagement by leadership and staff in both modalities, and to establish a strong foundation on which to build. Additional process measures identified were the completion of selected deliverables identified by the Steering Committee. Procedure The Steering Committee met monthly to direct the structure of the project and to support work teams in each modality. A charter was established, and KP’s Rapid Improvement Model served as a guide for goal development and change management. 14 The next step was administering baseline Safety Attitudes Questionnaires in early 2011. This was overseen by a project manager who was independent of the department to eliminate potential response bias. The KP National Environmental Health and Safety team provided data analysis support. The HRPT project was formally kicked off with human factors training for all staff and radiologists involved in procedures in both modalities. The modalities were closed down for a half day, and attendance was required. The service line administrative and physician leads attended the session to establish the case for the work and to show leadership support. KP leaders in patient safety adapted the surgical training to the specifics of imaging modalities for our project. Human factors training covered three topics. First was to build the case for HRPT using examples of errors and the resulting costs, including patient suffering, staff morale, and financial consequences. Second was to review the research on behaviors that have been shown to create risks to patient safety and clinical outcomes. This included a discussion of avoidable distractions and limits to human memory, as well as unresolved conflict and lack of respect among team members. The third topic was to define a highly reliable team and highly reliable processes. The overarching message was that “the goal is to take a team of experts and create an expert team.” 5 Following the training, the mammography and interventional radiology modalities established separate work teams to engage in targeted projects identified as best practices during previously implemented surgical safety work. The teams were directed to meet twice a month to build momentum and facilitate regular discussion of progress. The deliverables accomplished were as follows: solidifying all pieces of the time-out process, creating a poster of the checklist to reference in examination rooms, and implementing the new process for every procedure in both modalities. Ongoing projects included using “glitch books” (books used to capture glitches then document and track follow-up) for timely communication and follow-up on issues, use of whiteboards for patient information and flow, development and implementation of a monitoring tool, and an effective hand-off communication process. Work teams reported monthly to the Steering Committee. Interventional radiology’s time-out poster ( Figure 1 ) was posted and referenced in each procedure room. The poster clearly demarcated who was responsible for checking each element before the procedure and incorporated the patient in the time-out whenever possible. A debriefing process was also detailed. Use of this workflow was reinforced through monitoring and regular feedback. A similar poster was created for mammography procedures. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Highly Reliable Procedural Teams’ time-out poster for interventional radiology. DNR = do not resuscitate; KP = Kaiser Permanente; lab = laboratory; Med = medication; MR# = medical record number; prep = preparation; RN = registered nurse; Tech = technician. Results Safety Attitudes Questionnaire results from baseline in 2011 and follow-up in 2012 can be seen in Figure 2 . A greater than 90% response rate for staff and physicians was achieved for both modalities surveyed. At baseline, 92.9% of respondents in interventional radiology rated the teamwork climate “good,” and 50% rated the safety climate “good.” A score above 80% indicated a good culture. 15 , 16 At the 2012 follow-up, the interventional radiology modality’s teamwork climate rating decreased 5.4% and the safety climate rating increased 25%. In contrast, at baseline just 42.9% of mammography respondents rated the teamwork climate as good, and 42.9% rated the safety climate as good. At follow-up, the mammography modality’s teamwork climate rating decreased 16.6%, and the safety climate rating increased 4.5%. As is seen in other settings, there was a discrepancy between physician and staff perception of teamwork in both modalities, with physician ratings more than 15% higher in both areas. 17 , 18 Notably, the drop in teamwork climate in mammography was entirely driven by a decrease in staff perception, whereas physician perception remained the same. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Kaiser Permanente West Los Angeles Diagnostic Imaging Department’s teamwork and safety climate scores over time. Unusual Occurrence Report data from 2009, 2010, and 2011 showed 4, 10, and 11 items reported per respective year for the interventional radiology modality and 5, 0, and 5 items reported per year for mammography. Since kickoff, no sentinel events or near misses were reported in either modality. The goal continues to be accurate reporting of sentinel events and near misses, as well as to have zero sentinel events and minimal near misses in 2011 and beyond. The percentage of staff and physicians that attended human factors training was tracked. For both modalities, 94% of staff and 100% of radiologists attended the training. Critical event team training was more recently completed for the interventional radiology team. This was done using simulation, a recommended alternate way to improve teamwork and safety outcomes. Discussion The HRPT implementation journey at KP WLA led us down 2 disparate paths. At the outset, it was clear that the cultures in the mammography and interventional radiology modalities were very different, as the baseline Safety Attitudes Questionnaire data reflected. In particular, the teamwork climate varied greatly between the 2 groups (92.9% for interventional radiology and 42.9% for mammography). The interventional radiology team is historically a small and stable group, with strong internal leadership. The team’s culture has promoted effective communication among all team members, evidenced by the high baseline and follow-up results for teamwork climate. This culture, which has historically been a strength, revealed itself as a challenge during implementation. As Jim Collins 19 writes in Good to Great, “To go from good to great requires transcending the curse of competence.... Just because you’ve been doing it for years, or perhaps even decades does not necessarily mean you can be the best in the world at it.” The interventional radiology team’s strong self-perception of doing high-quality work became a hurdle for implementing the changes necessary to hardwire the highest level of safety. This is evidenced by inconsistently used checklists, debriefings, and glitch books as well as underreporting of near misses that continues after implementation. Safety climate scores increased to 75% because of HRPT efforts, and with continued focus should continue to move above the 80% benchmark for a good score. Before HRPT, the mammography staff voiced concern over their discomfort in speaking up around issues of patient safety. Human factors training provided the needed entrée to open lines of communication in this modality. This increased awareness of the shared responsibility to improve coordination and communication was likely the cause of the decreased teamwork scores, because inconsistent physician engagement continues to be a barrier in achieving true collaboration and has increased staff’s frustration. The safety climate scores remained stable, and as new members join the department, we are encouraged by their commitment to this work and their impact on future results of the Safety Attitudes Questionnaire. Although the issues in either modality are unique, a higher-level reason these presented as problems is that the program was initiated by senior leadership, rather than driven by departmental leadership. A complicating factor was that since kickoff of the project, the Diagnostic Imaging Department and both modalities have hired new managers. This turnover made it difficult to remain consistent in use of the improved processes. Most recently, the Steering Committee has reengaged around consistent audits, structured time-outs, and use of whiteboards. We look forward to the impact of our refined focus as the new managers and physicians engage around tightened requirements. In addition, as part of orientation, staff is oriented to the HRPT standards to ensure they are clear on expectations for procedures from Day 1. The Steering Committee believed that the baseline data for Unusual Occurrence Reports reflected an overall low level of reporting, a problem regularly discussed in the patient safety literature. 20 , 21 This is evidenced by near misses that have been discussed during Steering Committee meetings but continue to not be entered into our Unusual Occurrence Report system. Continued underreporting of unusual occurrences leaves us unaware of the true levels of errors and near misses. It has been communicated to staff that increased reporting of errors or near misses is evidence of a more open and just environment, and that the group’s intention is to focus on learning from mistakes in the system. Conclusion Implementation of the Universal Protocol in procedural areas in diagnostic imaging is increasingly important as the scope and volume expands. This work has shown that cultural change is paramount in achieving patient safety goals. Cultural change here includes physician and staff engagement in changing how work is done. Health care professionals must gain comfort with and a willingness to report, discuss, and learn from errors. Finally, health care teams should not allow a culture of good to hinder them from moving to a culture of great. Acknowledgments Stephen Lin, MD, led the implementation of the Highly Reliable Procedural Teams in the Kaiser Permanente West Los Angeles Diagnostic Imaging Department. Fredrick D Lee II, RT (R), MBA, led the implementation in his role as the Director of Diagnostic Imaging. Debra Osborn, RN, MPH, CPHQ, CPHRM, a Senior Nurse Consultant in Patient Safety at the Kaiser Permanente Southern California Regional Office assisted the team in translating the learnings from the Highly Reliable Surgical Teams project to allow for implementation in Diagnostic Imaging. She is involved in implementation of the Highly Reliable Procedural Team’s work in interventional radiology across Kaiser Permanente Southern California. Kathleen Louden, ELS, of Louden Health Communications provided editorial assistance. Footnotes Disclosure Statement The author(s) have no conflicts of interest to disclose. To Be Better Freedom is nothing but the chance to be better. —Albert Camus, 1913–1960, French Nobel Prize-winning author, journalist, and philosopher References 1. Universal Protocol [Internet] Oakbrook Terrace, IL: The Joint Commission; c2013. [cited 2013 Aug 28]. Available from: www.jointcommission.org/standards_information/up.aspx . [ Google Scholar ] 2. Angle JF, Nemcek AA, Jr, Cohen AM, et al. SIR Standards Division Quality improvement guidelines for preventing wrong site, wrong procedure, and wrong person errors: application of the joint commission “universal protocol for preventing wrong site, wrong procedure, wrong person surgery” to the practice of interventional radiology. J Vasc Interv Radiol. 2008 Aug;19(8):1145–51. doi: 10.1016/j.jvir.2008.03.027. DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jvir.2008.03.027 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Haynes AB, Weiser TG, Berry WR, et al. Safe Surgery Saves Lives Study Group A surgical safety checklist to reduce morbidity and mortality in a global population. N Engl J Med. 2009 Jan 29;360(5):491–9. doi: 10.1056/NEJMsa0810119. DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1056/NEJMsa0810119 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Applying the Universal Protocol to Improve Patient Safety in Radiology Services. Pennsylvania Patient Safety Advisory. 2011 Jun;8(2):63–9. [ Google Scholar ] 5. Convissar J, Retta C. Highly reliable procedural teams, Kaiser Permanente Medical Center. May 2011. 6. Mazzocco K, Petitti DB, Fong KT, et al. Surgical team behaviors and patient outcomes. Am J Surg. 2009 May;197(5):678–85. doi: 10.1016/j.amjsurg.2008.03.002. DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.amjsurg.2008.03.002 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Knight F, Galvin R, Davoren M, Mason KP. The evolution of the universal protocol in interventional radiology. J Radiol Nurs. 2006 Dec;25(4):106–15. DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jradnu.2006.08.003 . [ Google Scholar ] 8. Lee MJ, Fanelli F, Haage P, Hausegger K, Van Lienden KP. Patient safety in interventional radiology: a CIRSE IR checklist. Cardiovasc Intervent Radiol. 2012 Apr;35(2):244–6. doi: 10.1007/s00270-011-0289-5. DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s00270-011-0289-5 . [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Lewis DR, Bullbulia RA, Murphy P, et al. Vascular surgical intervention for complications of cardiovascular radiology: 13 years’ experience in a single centre. Ann R Coll Surg Engl. 1999 Jan;81(1):23–6. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Farris M, Anderson C, Doty S, Myers C, Johnson K, Prasad S. Non-OR procedural safety: health care protocol [Internet] Bloomington, MN: Institute for Clinical Systems Improvement; updated 2012 Sep [cited 2013 Aug 28]. Available from: www.icsi.org/_asset/1hht9h/NonOR-Interactive0912.pdf . [ Google Scholar ] 11. Reston, VA: American College of Radiology; ACR practice guideline for the performance of ultrasound-guided percutaneous breast interventional procedures (resolution 29) [Internet] revised 2009 [cited 2013 Aug 28]. Available from: www.acr.org/~/media/ACR/Documents/PGTS/guidelines/US_Guided_Breast.pdf . [ Google Scholar ] 12. Miller DL. Safety in interventional radiology. J Vasc Interv Radiol. 2007 Jan;18(1 Pt 1):1–3. doi: 10.1016/j.jvir.2006.10.007. DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jvir.2006.10.007 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Sexton JB, Helmreich RL, Neilands TB, et al. The Safety Attitudes Questionnaire: psychometric properties, benchmarking data, and emerging research. BMC Health Serv Res. 2006 Apr 3;6:44. doi: 10.1186/1472-6963-6-44. DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1186/1472-6963-6-44 . [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Schilling L, Dearing JW, Staley P, Harvey P, Fahey L, Kuruppu F. Kaiser Permanente’s performance improvement system, part 4: creating a learning organization. Jt Comm J Qual Patient Saf. 2011 Dec;37(12):532–43. doi: 10.1016/s1553-7250(11)37069-9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Pronovost P, Holzmueller CG, Needham DM, et al. How will we know patients are safer? An organization-wide approach to measuring and improving safety. Crit Care Med. 2006 Jul;34(7):1988–95. doi: 10.1097/01.CCM.0000226412.12612.B6. DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1097/01.CCM.0000226412.12612.B6 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Timmel J, Kent PS, Holzmueller CG, Paine L, Schulick RD, Pronovost PJ. Impact of the Comprehensive Unit-based Safety Program (CUSP) on safety culture in a surgical inpatient unit. Jt Comm J Qual Patient Saf. 2010 Jun;36(6):252–60. doi: 10.1016/s1553-7250(10)36040-5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Thomas EJ, Sexton JB, Helmreich RL. Discrepant attitudes about teamwork among critical care nurses and physicians. Crit Care Med. 2003 Mar;31(3):956–9. doi: 10.1097/01.CCM.0000056183.89175.76. DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1097/01.CCM.0000056183.89175.76 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Makary MA, Sexton JB, Freischlag JA, et al. Operating room teamwork among physicians and nurses: teamwork in the eye of the beholder. J Am Coll Surg. 2006 May;202(5):746–52. doi: 10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2006.01.017. DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2006.01.017 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Collins JC. Good to great: why some companies make the leap … and others don’t. New York, NY: HarperCollins Publishers Inc; 2001. [ Google Scholar ] 20. Committee on Quality of Health Care in America, Institute of Medicine . To err is human: building a safer health system. Washington, DC: National Academies Press; 1999. Nov, [ Google Scholar ] 21. Paterick ZR, Paterick BB, Waterhouse BE, Paterick TE. The challenges to transparency in reporting medical errors. J Patient Saf. 2009 Dec;5(4):205–9. doi: 10.1097/PTS.0b013e3181be2a88. DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1097/PTS.0b013e3181be2a88 . [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',12,'2025-10-15 10:13:04','2025-10-15 10:13:04');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4778,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10950100','Artificial intelligence diagnosis based on breast ultrasound imaging.','Lan Z, Peng Y.','2022','乳腺癌目前已成为女性第一大癌症 研发乳腺超声人工智能 诊断技术有利于推进中国乳腺癌的精准诊疗 缓解地区发展不平衡下沉重的医疗负担 近年来 超声ai技术在提高诊断效能的基础上 不断与多种乳腺超声新兴技术进行结合 以适应各种临床应用场景 从而为临床决策提供更加全面可靠的循证依据 虽然基于传统乳腺超声灰阶图像的ai诊断技术以及三维成像 弹性成像等前沿技术图像得到一定发展 但乳腺超声ai诊断技术的开发中仍存在技术痛点 推广难点和伦理困境 乳腺超声检查 人工智能 计算机辅助 多模态成像 医学图像融合 2021年世界卫生组织国际癌症研究机构 发布的最新数据显示 乳腺癌全球新发病例数达226万例 占所有癌症发病率的11 已超过肺癌 约220万例 成为世界癌症发病率的第一位 而在全球女性癌症病死率排名中乳腺癌也位居第一 约68万例 得益于早期筛查的普及 全球乳腺癌病死率已呈下降趋势 然而中国由于地区发展不平衡 医疗资源配置参差不齐 乳腺癌病死率还在逐年增长 超声作为中国女性乳腺检查的首选 提升其诊断效能对控制中国严峻的乳腺癌形势意义重大 而人工智能 为此提供了高效的解决办法 自首次提出ai概念后的60多年来 ai技术在各领域都得到广泛研究 并在医学尤其是医学影像领域取得了突破性进展 乳腺疾病的临床诊疗在很大程度上依赖着医学影像 加上影像储存和传输系统 munication 的推广 数量可观的数据库逐渐形成 而医疗程序的规范化也为算法介入创造了先决条件 医学影像ai因此得以在乳腺诊疗上蓬勃发展并不断展现出新活力 相较于具有放射性且对致密型乳腺敏感度低的钼靶和十分昂贵且配置紧缺的mri 超声更符合中国人口基数大的实情 因此大力推进乳腺超声ai诊断技术的研发势在必行 笔者基于乳腺超声图像的ai诊断技术的最新研究进展作一综述 并分析当前面临的挑战和突破口 以期为学者未来研究超声ai技术提供思路 乳腺超声计算机辅助检测 诊断模型 计算机辅助检测 模型运用机器学习 经过图像分割 特征提取 量化分析等步骤对医学影像中的可疑病灶作出判断 它的出现推进了影像诊断从强主观性 依靠感知技能的阶段向更客观 更准确 可重复的新阶段转变 cad模型可分为基于传统ml和基于深度学习 前者由人工完成图像表型特征的提取 后者训练更为复杂的算法由网络自行提炼特征信息 基于传统 早期的乳腺超声cad模型在构建时依赖人工编码图像特征 这些特征主要依据乳腺成像报告和数据系统 术语辞典 一种为了提高医师一致性形成的记录 评估超声图像指标的描述体系 进行算法定义 再通过ml对经由不同算法产生的大量图像特征数据进行选择和缩减 最后达到处理分析未知数据的目的 这类模型的运算复杂性较低 使用较便捷 可解释性较强 近年来仍受到许多关注和研究 为了提高ml cad模型的诊断效能 学者们 就特征组合方式开展了许多探索 发现病灶边缘和方位特征对模型的贡献最佳 且这些模型诊断表现良好 曲线下面积 在进行bi rads分类时也能有效辅助医师作出判断 作为最早结合乳腺医学影像的ai模型 首个ml模型b 加拿大medipattern公司 早在2005年就获得了fda批准在美国上市 虽然稍晚于钼靶 但其分类性能好 不容易发生过度拟合 具有较高的鲁棒性 然而这类模型特异度较低 可能造成过度医疗 针对这个弱点 cristerna等 构建了将肿瘤分类为 可能良性 的bi rads加权cad模型 可以高效区分后两类肿瘤 从而提供更客观的随访及活体组织检查 以下简称活检 在2022年的最新报道 一种经过内部交叉检验及外部检验的cad模型在保证高敏感度的前提下 使15 以上的良性病变避免了不必要的活检 基于ml算法下的超声纹理特征的价值也令人瞩目 该特征被证实可以代替肉眼察觉4a类良恶性病变之间的细微差异 既可以有效鉴别三阴性乳腺癌 tnbc 和纤维腺瘤 还可以鉴别良性叶状肿瘤和交界性恶性叶状肿瘤 从而为临床决策提供可靠建议 改善患者的预后 dl旨在模仿人脑神经网络结构 用逐层训练的方式使计算机自行对带标签的大数据集进行学习 发现数据的内部规律并完成模型构建 卷积神经网络 是使用最多 研究最成功的dl算法结构 其核心是卷积层 该层模拟人脑识别图像并提取特征 随后的池化层则对大量特征数据进行降维 从而提高模型的鲁棒性及泛化能力 由ml发展而来的dl进一步减轻了人工负担 使cad模型研究进入了新阶段并迅猛发展 相较于早期研究 近年的dl cad模型训练集样本量大幅增加 几乎都能达到千张以上 且在近3年尤为明显 这些模型无一例外都展现了良好的诊断表现 有的甚至超越了高年资医师 这些研究为提升模型的效能提供了许多参考 肿瘤边缘与勾画边缘之间的宽度也会影响模型性能 最佳边距 准确度 可能是180像素 传统的resnet50网络容易遗漏小病灶 li等 将其改良后的模型在近8 000张的数据集中应用 结果显示其敏感度 和特异度 还有学者 创新性地将cnn与传统ml算法联合 使超声cad模型的良 恶性分类能力 直接赶上了以同样方式构建起来的动态增强mri cad模型 在2021年出现了一种新观点 即弱监督dl算法也能较好地完成病灶的定位 且与全监督dl算法无显著区别 为了满足临床bi rads分类的需要 huang等 将dl模型分为肿瘤识别和分类2个阶段 分别对应感兴趣区 cnn训练 5类肿块分类时准确度 均明显优于超声医师 而ciritsis等 则应用深度卷积神经网络 在包括2 5类病灶的数据集中进行模型训练 外部测试集中的auc分别达到0 他们通过热图将模型提取区域 分类特征可视化 有效克服了dl的 虽然部分dl cad模型现已上市 其中较早的乳腺s 韩国samsung medison公司 软件在近几年的研究中对欧美女性也表现出了良好的诊断效能 但此类产品目前只作为缺乏经验者的参考或教学工具 除诊断乳腺癌外 近期的研究也展现了乳腺超声dl模型在预测分子分型 淋巴结转移以及新辅助化学药物治疗 以下简称化疗 效果等方面的应用前景 stoffel等 开发了一种用于区分叶状肿瘤和纤维腺瘤的模型 但其未能明显提升医师的诊断效能 zhang等 在2021年报道 构建的dl模型不仅实现了诊断4a类肿块的高准确度 还在预测tnbc的分子亚型时将auc最高提升至0 在acosog z0011实验后时代 许多超声ai研究者也开始研发术前无创评估乳腺癌腋窝淋巴结转移的模型 其中zhou等 在2019年首次构建了能够有效预测 早期淋巴结转移的cnn模型 同年另一研究 也证实dl模型可以检测出超声图像中因淋巴结转移所带来的微观结构成分改变 从而将医师的诊断准确度提高到87 之后还有学者 结合常规超声与剪切波弹性成像 在预测是否存在腋窝淋巴结转移的基础上 实现了淋巴结负担程度的区分 在预测新辅助化疗效果方面 最近的研究 发现联合形态与神经网络特征可以显著提高超声dl模型的早期预测表现 这为患者得到及时准确的疗效评估提供了新思路 乳腺超声新技术与 自动化乳腺超声成像 乳腺三维 成像设备的开发将超声医师从传统手持超声中解放出来 为提高检查一致性 可重复性开辟了新的道路 其中自动乳腺全容积扫描 scaning abvs 继承了反射波成像原理 而超声断层扫描 则运用超声波透射成像原理 二者均能得到连续断层图像 并依靠强大的后处理技术获取到远多于常规检查的图像信息 abvs可以弥补常规超声钙化点检出率低的弱点 但每次检查都会生成数百张图像 由此带来的读片负担可能会导致超声医师疲劳及漏诊率升高 为解决上述问题 zelst等 在2017年借助dl cad软件辅助abvs诊断 成功将低年资医师的auc从0 77提高到0 在他们次年的报道 进一步指出cad软件可以提高医师的特异度 同时不影响其敏感度 理论上abvs cad软件可以代替abvs联合钼靶的扫查协议 在abvs二维切面图像上手动标注roi能使ml模型在学习分析47个乳腺纹理特征后识别乳腺病变的准确度达90 auc达到0 9以上 但为了更大限度地发挥3d容积成像的优势 chiang等 提出在滑动窗口中提取感兴趣体区域 以此训练优化3d cnn对可疑肿瘤进行自动识别 这在提高时间效率的同时也保证了良好的检测性能 2022年 hejduk等 基于dcnn算法也在滑动窗口中完成了病灶自动检测以及bi rads分类 但xiao等 考虑到灵敏度与假阳性的权衡 没有使用滑动窗口而是通过改进3d net架构实现了对 肿瘤的灵敏度检测 ust具有高空间分辨率 在显示乳腺结构 纤维基质 cooper韧带等 以及恶性肿瘤组织 特征性血管分布 不规则轮廓等 时有着突出表现 值得注意的是 早有研究 证明乳腺密度是乳腺癌的独立风险指标之一 ust理论上可以代替x线 对乳腺密度进行自动评估 在未来制订乳腺个体化诊疗方案时ust或将发挥重要作用 而目前相关研究尚处于起步阶段 ust图像解读不同于当下任何常见的医学影像设备的标准图像 医师需要接受额外培训才能准确读图 或许直接通过ai提取图像信息才是学者们的明智选择 乳腺超声多模态成像 在实际临床工作中 超声医师诊断乳腺病灶时还要综合彩色多普勒血流成像 弹性成像 对比增强超声 等多种成像模式结果 将这些多模态成像技术与乳腺超声ai进行结合 也能进一步提高模型的诊断性能 se属于定量超声 qus 具有多参数性 便于ai模型的构建 zhang等 率先使用层次聚类并基于3种指标选择特征 筛选出了7个高吞吐量的弹性特征建模 最终能高敏感度 和高特异度 地区分乳腺良恶性肿瘤 2022年的最新报道 指出emax参数是对bi 4类病灶诊断效果最佳 的swe se参数 qus中的射频 回波信号也被用来设计多参数融合算法 实现了乳腺肿块钙化灶的高精度 准确度88 自动检测 为了提高效率 yu等 在swe图像上实现了自动分割roi fujioka等 运用cnn算法进行自动诊断 dl算法也开始在swe se研究中崭露头角 2021年报道 的一种结合se与灰阶超声的dl cad组合模型在区别良恶性病灶时特异度高达91 证明多模态集成能够克服单一模态cad模型的部分缺点 在cdfi与灰阶超声联合建模的尝试中 wu等 发现联合模型将大幅提升对tnbc和非tnbc的亚型诊断性能 敏感度 特异度 qian等 通过上万例的数据集训练了一种双模态dl cad模型 结果显示在bi rads分类时该模型 比灰阶模型 与医师的一致性更好 而在鉴别良恶性时 联合模型的表现 更是超过了超声医师 liang等 在2021年基于年龄 灰阶及多普勒特征构建了多层感知器模型 可以有效鉴别黏液性乳腺癌与纤维腺瘤 截至本文定稿时还未检索到应用于乳腺的ceus cad模型报道 随着诊断设备的全面数字化 多模态超声技术仍在不断革新 其意义不再局限于对传统超声弱点的补足 更重要的是其中潜在的肿瘤生物学信息 这将为拓宽超声影像组学研究思路提供关键方向 图像融合技术 mri和pet等医学成像技术在分别对病变进行诊断时各有其优劣势 图像融合技术综合各种成像技术的优势 具有可将各种技术进行配准融合显示 在乳腺癌的图像融合技术中 与超声融合的通常是mri 当mri首次检出超声检查结果为阴性的乳腺肿瘤后 通过mri 超声融合成像可以对此类肿瘤实现超声引导下穿刺活检 能提高工作效率 降低医疗成本和患者的不适感 而在对此类肿瘤随访时 超声融合技术也使得复查时超声影像检查变得可行 并达到对病灶的 辅助医师制订术前计划 abvs可以提高超声成像的标准化程度 从而降低图像融合时的计算难度 基于abvs结合钼靶使用的共识 学者们近年来在数字化乳腺断层合成 与abvs融合成像上进行了尝试 green等 运用自动形变配准的方法 首次使2种3d图像可以在不进行外部基准标记患者皮肤的情况下完成配准 这对较复杂的乳腺病变 致密型乳腺 多发肿块 的检出和定位很有价值 目前已有一些dbt abvs融合成像产品得到了fda的批准 但还仅用于研究 除上述2种组合外 2021年的一篇个案报道 首次使用实时超声 ct融合成像虚拟导航技术来检测 定位乳腺癌病灶 这是超声融合pet ct成像应用于乳腺疾病的全新尝试 如果把不同的成像技术看作不同的感官 那么图像融合就可以看作是模拟人脑对多感官信息的整合分析 如何提高不同模式乳腺病灶图像之间的空间配准 是未来乳腺领域图像融合技术发展的关键所在 挑战与展望 国内外涌现了的大量乳腺超声ai诊断方面的研究文献 但要打破 实验室 实现超声ai诊断技术从理论到临床的跨越 学者们仍面临着诸多挑战 算法技术瓶颈的突破 现有的乳腺超声ai 无论是已有广泛报道的基于灰阶图像的模型 还是尚处于研究起步阶段的结合各类超声新技术开发的模型 都存在以下问题 由于ml模型较依赖人工 且学术界在图像特征 ml分类技术的最优组合等方面尚未达成共识 该类模型存在效率较低 主观性较高 容易忽略潜在特征等问题 dl算法的出现将图像特征提取过程自主化 这虽然削弱了模型对人工的依赖 但也无法避免地带来的透明度低 特征解释正确性不明等问题 如何权衡训练数据集 roi的大小与训练算法的时间成本 如何在性能更佳的dl方法和可解释性更强 成本更低的传统ml方法中进行选择 为防止发生过度拟合如何识别并去除冗余特征 且不降低诊断效能等问题都是亟待解决的难题 除此之外 在实际临床工作中 除了超声图像表现 超声医师还要综合患者的病史及其他检查结果才能作出最合理的诊断建议 为了突破当下的研究瓶颈 还应该开发结合其他相关非影像学信息的超声ai模型 实验设计标准化与研究成果推广 乳腺超声ai相关研究虽多 部分产品已获得fda批准上市 但能真正推广应用的成果却凤毛麟角 这不仅与建模技术本身的差异化有关 还与实验流程的科学性和异质性有关 因为医学影像数据的特殊性 已有研究大多都是小样本 单中心 回顾性的 国内外目前都缺乏可共享的结构化标准数据库 部分模型研究未严格设定3个独立的训练集 验证集和测试集 也因此造成了偏倚和过拟合 特征提取的方法或系统缺乏标准 也会导致实验的可重复性差 不同科研团队难以对同一特征或系统展开研究 ai性能评价指标不统一 测试及验证方式也缺乏规范 伦理争议的困境 如同其他医疗ai技术 乳腺超声ai也面临着许多法律 道德难题 无论是构建ai诊断预测系统还是提升其性能 都对大量 全面的数据库有着高度依赖 有效防止患者隐私泄露 是实现医疗数据共享之前必须解决的问题 即使在超声ai的辅助下也难以完全避免误诊与漏诊 由此产生的过失如何界定责任 还需要政府部门不断完善相应的法律法规 ai的介入定将改变传统医患关系 超声医师必须适应新的角色定位 维护与患者之间的良好互动 回顾超声ai诊断技术近些年的蓬勃发展 虽然新技术的出现不可避免地伴随着许多争议和挑战 但不可否定的是身处大数据时代 研发乳腺超声ai是顺应时代潮流的必然选择 更是一条解决当下我国乳腺癌严峻形势的新出路 为了早日实现超声ai诊断技术投入临床使用 学者们要立足于当下已有的理论 技术和资源基础 着眼于数据库共享 算法模型优化 特征提取标准化等方面 不断凝聚共识 寻求突破 方能朝着这个最终目标不断迈进 基金资助 成都市科技项目 cy02 cy02 利益冲突声明 作者声称无任何利益冲突 作者贡献 兰梓涵 文献检索与整理 撰写与修改 彭玉兰 论文指导 所有作者阅读并同意最终的文本 原文网址 xbyxb csu xbwk fileup 参考文献 张保宁 陈万青 中国乳腺癌防控形势面临挑战 中华肿瘤杂志 baoning wangqing ultrasmedbio cadrin chenevert carj svn bitencourt daimiel gullo garra ultrasmedbio ultrasmedbio 王霄英 计算机辅助诊断在医学影像诊断中的基本原理和应用进展 放射学实践 shuai xiaying cristerna cmpb interlenghi salvatore magni diagnostics12010187 fibroadenoma fibroadenomas basara ozgul altay phyllodes phyllodes ultraschall korfiatis akkus lecun nature14539 s12938 ciritsis eberhard stoffel wurnig phyllodes fibroadenoma ejro aa82ec antropova giger bartolotta ganau ultrasmedbio byra dobruch sobczak klimonda siamese jbhi skaane gullien eben abus opielinski pruchnicki szymanowski compmedimag zelst platel zelst clauser marcon ciritsis s41747 hejduk marcon unkelbach abus wiskin natesan bovbjerg sonoelastomics sonoelastography ultrasmedbio qims embc supersonic compbiomed fujioka katsuta kubota ultrason managuli ultrason ferroelectr freq tuffc multilayer perceptron fibroadenoma paparo piccazzo cevasco ultrason s10396 uematsu goodsitt roubidoux deformable kubota fujioka kaunas medicina57121289','Abstract 乳腺癌目前已成为女性第一大癌症。研发乳腺超声人工智能(artificial intelligence，AI)诊断技术有利于推进中国乳腺癌的精准诊疗，缓解地区发展不平衡下沉重的医疗负担。近年来，超声AI技术在提高诊断效能的基础上，不断与多种乳腺超声新兴技术进行结合，以适应各种临床应用场景，从而为临床决策提供更加全面可靠的循证依据。虽然基于传统乳腺超声灰阶图像的AI诊断技术以及三维成像、弹性成像等前沿技术图像得到一定发展，但乳腺超声AI诊断技术的开发中仍存在技术痛点、推广难点和伦理困境。 Keywords: 乳腺超声检查, 人工智能, 计算机辅助, 多模态成像, 医学图像融合 Abstract Breast cancer has now become the leading cancer in women. The development of breast ultrasound artificial intelligence (AI) diagnostic technology is conducive to promoting the precise diagnosis and treatment of breast cancer and alleviating the heavy medical burden due to the unbalanced regional development in China. In recent years, on the basis of improving diagnostic efficiency, AI technology has been continuously combined with various clinical application scenarios, thereby providing more comprehensive and reliable evidence-based suggestions for clinical decision-making. Although AI diagnostic technologies based on conventional breast ultrasound gray-scale images and cutting-edge technologies such as three-dimensional (3D) imaging and elastography have been developed to some extent, there are still technical pain points, diffusion difficulties and ethical dilemmas in the development of AI diagnostic technologies for breast ultrasound. Keywords: breast ultrasonography, artificial intelligence, computer assistant, multimodal imaging, medical image fusion 2021年世界卫生组织国际癌症研究机构(International Agency for Research on Cancer，IARC)发布的最新数据显示：乳腺癌全球新发病例数达226万例，占所有癌症发病率的11.7%，已超过肺癌(约220万例，11.4%)成为世界癌症发病率的第一位；而在全球女性癌症病死率排名中乳腺癌也位居第一(约68万例，15.3%) [ 1 ] 。得益于早期筛查的普及，全球乳腺癌病死率已呈下降趋势。然而中国由于地区发展不平衡、医疗资源配置参差不齐，乳腺癌病死率还在逐年增长 [ 2 ] 。超声作为中国女性乳腺检查的首选，提升其诊断效能对控制中国严峻的乳腺癌形势意义重大 [ 3 ] ，而人工智能(artificial intelligence，AI)为此提供了高效的解决办法。自首次提出AI概念后的60多年来，AI技术在各领域都得到广泛研究，并在医学尤其是医学影像领域取得了突破性进展 [ 4 ] 。乳腺疾病的临床诊疗在很大程度上依赖着医学影像，加上影像储存和传输系统(picture archiving and com-munication system，PACS)的推广，数量可观的数据库逐渐形成，而医疗程序的规范化也为算法介入创造了先决条件，医学影像AI因此得以在乳腺诊疗上蓬勃发展并不断展现出新活力。相较于具有放射性且对致密型乳腺敏感度低的钼靶和十分昂贵且配置紧缺的MRI，超声更符合中国人口基数大的实情，因此大力推进乳腺超声AI诊断技术的研发势在必行。笔者基于乳腺超声图像的AI诊断技术的最新研究进展作一综述，并分析当前面临的挑战和突破口，以期为学者未来研究超声AI技术提供思路。 1. 乳腺超声计算机辅助检测 / 诊断模型 计算机辅助检测/诊断(computer-aided detection/diagnosis，CAD)模型运用机器学习(machine learning，ML)经过图像分割、特征提取、量化分析等步骤对医学影像中的可疑病灶作出判断 [ 5 ] 。它的出现推进了影像诊断从强主观性、依靠感知技能的阶段向更客观、更准确、可重复的新阶段转变 [ 6 ] 。CAD模型可分为基于传统ML和基于深度学习(deep learning，DL)2类，前者由人工完成图像表型特征的提取，后者训练更为复杂的算法由网络自行提炼特征信息。 1.1. 基于传统 ML 的 CAD 模型 早期的乳腺超声CAD模型在构建时依赖人工编码图像特征，这些特征主要依据乳腺成像报告和数据系统(breast imaging-reporting and data system，BI-RADS)术语辞典(一种为了提高医师一致性形成的记录、评估超声图像指标的描述体系)进行算法定义，再通过ML对经由不同算法产生的大量图像特征数据进行选择和缩减，最后达到处理分析未知数据的目的 [ 5 ] 。这类模型的运算复杂性较低、使用较便捷、可解释性较强，近年来仍受到许多关注和研究 [ 6 ] 。 为了提高ML-CAD模型的诊断效能，学者们 [ 7 - 10 ] 就特征组合方式开展了许多探索，发现病灶边缘和方位特征对模型的贡献最佳，且这些模型诊断表现良好[曲线下面积(area under the curve，AUC)：0.87~0.97]，在进行BI-RADS分类时也能有效辅助医师作出判断 [ 9 - 10 ] 。作为最早结合乳腺医学影像的AI模型，首个ML模型B-CAD(加拿大Medipattern公司)早在2005年就获得了FDA批准在美国上市，虽然稍晚于钼靶，但其分类性能好，不容易发生过度拟合，具有较高的鲁棒性 [ 11 ] 。然而这类模型特异度较低，可能造成过度医疗。针对这个弱点，Rodríguez-Cristerna等 [ 12 ] 构建了将肿瘤分类为“良性、可能良性、恶性”的BI-RADS加权CAD模型，可以高效区分后两类肿瘤(AUC：0.86)，从而提供更客观的随访及活体组织检查(以下简称活检)建议。在2022年的最新报道 [ 13 ] 中，一种经过内部交叉检验及外部检验的CAD模型在保证高敏感度的前提下，使15%以上的良性病变避免了不必要的活检。此外，基于ML算法下的超声纹理特征的价值也令人瞩目。该特征被证实可以代替肉眼察觉4a类良恶性病变之间的细微差异 [ 14 ] ，既可以有效鉴别三阴性乳腺癌(triple-negative breast cancer，TNBC)和纤维腺瘤(AUC：0.85~0.97) [ 15 - 16 ] ，还可以鉴别良性叶状肿瘤和交界性恶性叶状肿瘤，从而为临床决策提供可靠建议，改善患者的预后 [ 17 ] 。 1.2. 基于 DL 的 CAD 模型 DL旨在模仿人脑神经网络结构，用逐层训练的方式使计算机自行对带标签的大数据集进行学习，发现数据的内部规律并完成模型构建 [ 18 ] 。目前，卷积神经网络(convolutional neural network，CNN)是使用最多、研究最成功的DL算法结构 [ 6 ] 。其核心是卷积层，该层模拟人脑识别图像并提取特征；随后的池化层则对大量特征数据进行降维，从而提高模型的鲁棒性及泛化能力 [ 19 ] 。由ML发展而来的DL进一步减轻了人工负担，使CAD模型研究进入了新阶段并迅猛发展。 相较于早期研究，近年的DL-CAD模型训练集样本量大幅增加，几乎都能达到千张以上，且在近3年尤为明显 [ 20 - 22 ] 。这些模型无一例外都展现了良好的诊断表现，有的甚至超越了高年资医师 [ 22 - 23 ] 。这些研究为提升模型的效能提供了许多参考，比如：肿瘤边缘与勾画边缘之间的宽度也会影响模型性能，最佳边距(AUC：0.96；准确度：89.86%)可能是180像素 [ 24 ] 。传统的ResNet50网络容易遗漏小病灶，Li等 [ 22 ] 将其改良后的模型在近8 000张的数据集中应用，结果显示其敏感度(92%)和特异度(90%)俱佳。还有学者 [ 25 ] 创新性地将CNN与传统ML算法联合，使超声CAD模型的良、恶性分类能力(AUC：0.90)直接赶上了以同样方式构建起来的动态增强MRI(dynamic contrast enhanced-MRI，DCE-MRI)CAD模型(AUC：0.89)。在2021年出现了一种新观点，即弱监督DL算法也能较好地完成病灶的定位、诊断(AUC：0.86~0.96)，且与全监督DL算法无显著区别 [ 26 ] 。为了满足临床BI-RADS分类的需要，Huang等 [ 20 ] 将DL模型分为肿瘤识别和分类2个阶段，分别对应感兴趣区(regions of interest，ROI)-CNN、图像(Graph，G)-CNN训练，在3~5类肿块分类时准确度(0.73~0.90)均明显优于超声医师。而Ciritsis等 [ 21 ] 则应用深度卷积神经网络(deep CNN，dCNN)在包括2~5类病灶的数据集中进行模型训练，在内、外部测试集中的AUC分别达到0.84、0.87；同时，他们通过热图将模型提取区域、分类特征可视化，有效克服了DL的“黑盒”特性。虽然部分DL-CAD模型现已上市，其中较早的乳腺S-Detect TM (韩国Samsung Medison公司)软件在近几年的研究中对欧美女性也表现出了良好的诊断效能 [ 27 ] ，但此类产品目前只作为缺乏经验者的参考或教学工具。 除诊断乳腺癌外，近期的研究也展现了乳腺超声DL模型在预测分子分型、淋巴结转移以及新辅助化学药物治疗(以下简称化疗)效果等方面的应用前景。Stoffel等 [ 23 ] 开发了一种用于区分叶状肿瘤和纤维腺瘤的模型，但其未能明显提升医师的诊断效能。Zhang等 [ 28 ] 在2021年报道：构建的DL模型不仅实现了诊断4a类肿块的高准确度(92.86%)，还在预测TNBC的分子亚型时将AUC最高提升至0.86。在ACOSOG Z0011实验后时代，许多超声AI研究者也开始研发术前无创评估乳腺癌腋窝淋巴结转移的模型，其中Zhou等 [ 29 ] 在2019年首次构建了能够有效预测(AUC：0.90)早期淋巴结转移的CNN模型；同年另一研究 [ 30 ] 也证实DL模型可以检测出超声图像中因淋巴结转移所带来的微观结构成分改变，从而将医师的诊断准确度提高到87.0%；之后还有学者 [ 31 ] 结合常规超声与剪切波弹性成像(shear wave elastography，SWE)建模，在预测是否存在腋窝淋巴结转移的基础上(AUC：0.90)实现了淋巴结负担程度的区分(AUC：0.91)。在预测新辅助化疗效果方面，最近的研究 [ 32 ] 发现联合形态与神经网络特征可以显著提高超声DL模型的早期预测表现(AUC：0.84)，这为患者得到及时准确的疗效评估提供了新思路。 2. 乳腺超声新技术与 AI 2.1. 自动化乳腺超声成像 乳腺三维(three dimensions，3D)成像设备的开发将超声医师从传统手持超声中解放出来，为提高检查一致性、可重复性开辟了新的道路。其中自动乳腺全容积扫描(automated breast volume scaning，ABVS)继承了反射波成像原理 [ 33 ] ；而超声断层扫描 (ultrasound tomography，UST)则运用超声波透射成像原理 [ 34 ] 。二者均能得到连续断层图像，并依靠强大的后处理技术获取到远多于常规检查的图像信息。 ABVS可以弥补常规超声钙化点检出率低的弱点，但每次检查都会生成数百张图像，由此带来的读片负担可能会导致超声医师疲劳及漏诊率升高 [ 33 ] 。为解决上述问题，van Zelst等 [ 35 ] 在2017年借助DL-CAD软件辅助ABVS诊断，成功将低年资医师的AUC从0.77提高到0.84；在他们次年的报道 [ 36 ] 中，进一步指出CAD软件可以提高医师的特异度，同时不影响其敏感度。因此，理论上ABVS-CAD软件可以代替ABVS联合钼靶的扫查协议 [ 33 ] 。在ABVS二维切面图像上手动标注ROI能使ML模型在学习分析47个乳腺纹理特征后识别乳腺病变的准确度达90%，AUC达到0.9以上 [ 37 ] 。但为了更大限度地发挥3D容积成像的优势，Chiang等 [ 38 ] 提出在滑动窗口中提取感兴趣体区域(volumes of interest，VOI)，以此训练优化3D CNN对可疑肿瘤进行自动识别，这在提高时间效率的同时也保证了良好的检测性能。2022年，Hejduk等 [ 39 ] 基于dCNN算法也在滑动窗口中完成了病灶自动检测以及BI-RADS分类(AUC：0.91)。但Xiao等 [ 40 ] 考虑到灵敏度与假阳性的权衡，没有使用滑动窗口而是通过改进3D U-net架构实现了对≤1 cm 3 肿瘤的灵敏度检测。 UST具有高空间分辨率，在显示乳腺结构(纤维基质、Cooper韧带等)以及恶性肿瘤组织(特征性血管分布、不规则轮廓等)时有着突出表现 [ 41 ] 。值得注意的是，早有研究 [ 42 ] 证明乳腺密度是乳腺癌的独立风险指标之一。UST理论上可以代替X线、MRI，对乳腺密度进行自动评估 [ 41 ] ，在未来制订乳腺个体化诊疗方案时UST或将发挥重要作用，而目前相关研究尚处于起步阶段。UST图像解读不同于当下任何常见的医学影像设备的标准图像，医师需要接受额外培训才能准确读图，或许直接通过AI提取图像信息才是学者们的明智选择。 2.2. 乳腺超声多模态成像 在实际临床工作中，超声医师诊断乳腺病灶时还要综合彩色多普勒血流成像(color doppler flow imaging，CDFI)、弹性成像(shear wave elastography/strain elastography，SWE/SE)、对比增强超声(contrast-enhanced ultrasound，CEUS)等多种成像模式结果。将这些多模态成像技术与乳腺超声AI进行结合，也能进一步提高模型的诊断性能。 SWE/SE属于定量超声(quantitative ultrasound，QUS)，具有多参数性，便于AI模型的构建。Zhang等 [ 43 ] 率先使用层次聚类并基于3种指标选择特征，筛选出了7个高吞吐量的弹性特征建模，最终能高敏感度(85.7%)和高特异度(89.3%)地区分乳腺良恶性肿瘤。2022年的最新报道 [ 44 ] 指出Emax参数是对BI-RADS 4类病灶诊断效果最佳(AUC：0.91)的SWE/SE参数。另外，QUS中的射频(radio frequency，RF)回波信号也被用来设计多参数融合算法，实现了乳腺肿块钙化灶的高精度(准确度88%)自动检测 [ 45 ] 。为了提高效率，Yu等 [ 46 ] 在SWE图像上实现了自动分割ROI，Fujioka等 [ 47 ] 运用CNN算法进行自动诊断(AUC：0.87)，DL算法也开始在SWE/SE研究中崭露头角。2021年报道 [ 48 ] 的一种结合SE与灰阶超声的DL-CAD组合模型在区别良恶性病灶时特异度高达91.10%，证明多模态集成能够克服单一模态CAD模型的部分缺点。 在CDFI与灰阶超声联合建模的尝试中，Wu等 [ 49 ] 发现联合模型将大幅提升对TNBC和非TNBC的亚型诊断性能(AUC：0.88； 敏感度：0.87；特异度：0.83)。Qian等 [ 50 ] 通过上万例的数据集训练了一种双模态DL-CAD模型，结果显示在BI-RADS分类时该模型(κ：0.73)比灰阶模型(κ: 0.62)与医师的一致性更好；而在鉴别良恶性时，联合模型的表现(AUC: 0.98)更是超过了超声医师(AUC：0.95)。Liang等 [ 51 ] 在2021年基于年龄、灰阶及多普勒特征构建了多层感知器模型，可以有效鉴别黏液性乳腺癌与纤维腺瘤(AUC：0.92)。截至本文定稿时还未检索到应用于乳腺的CEUS-CAD模型报道。 随着诊断设备的全面数字化，多模态超声技术仍在不断革新。其意义不再局限于对传统超声弱点的补足，更重要的是其中潜在的肿瘤生物学信息，这将为拓宽超声影像组学研究思路提供关键方向。 2.3. 图像融合技术 超声、钼靶、MRI和PET等医学成像技术在分别对病变进行诊断时各有其优劣势，图像融合技术综合各种成像技术的优势，具有可将各种技术进行配准融合显示 [ 52 ] 。在乳腺癌的图像融合技术中，与超声融合的通常是MRI。当MRI首次检出超声检查结果为阴性的乳腺肿瘤后，通过MRI/超声融合成像可以对此类肿瘤实现超声引导下穿刺活检，能提高工作效率，降低医疗成本和患者的不适感 [ 53 ] 。而在对此类肿瘤随访时，MRI/超声融合技术也使得复查时超声影像检查变得可行，并达到对病灶的“精准”检测，辅助医师制订术前计划 [ 54 ] 。 ABVS可以提高超声成像的标准化程度，从而降低图像融合时的计算难度。基于ABVS结合钼靶使用的共识 [ 42 ] ，学者们近年来在数字化乳腺断层合成(digital breast tomosynthesis，DBT)与ABVS融合成像上进行了尝试。Green等 [ 55 ] 运用自动形变配准的方法，首次使2种3D图像可以在不进行外部基准标记患者皮肤的情况下完成配准，这对较复杂的乳腺病变(致密型乳腺、多发肿块)的检出和定位很有价值。目前已有一些DBT/ABVS融合成像产品得到了FDA的批准，但还仅用于研究。 除上述2种组合外，2021年的一篇个案报道 [ 56 ] 首次使用实时超声/ 18 F-FDG PET/CT融合成像虚拟导航技术来检测、定位乳腺癌病灶，这是超声融合PET/CT成像应用于乳腺疾病的全新尝试。如果把不同的成像技术看作不同的感官，那么图像融合就可以看作是模拟人脑对多感官信息的整合分析。如何提高不同模式乳腺病灶图像之间的空间配准，是未来乳腺领域图像融合技术发展的关键所在。 3. 挑战与展望 目前，国内外涌现了的大量乳腺超声AI诊断方面的研究文献，但要打破“实验室”效应，实现超声AI诊断技术从理论到临床的跨越，学者们仍面临着诸多挑战。 3.1. 算法技术瓶颈的突破 现有的乳腺超声AI，无论是已有广泛报道的基于灰阶图像的模型，还是尚处于研究起步阶段的结合各类超声新技术开发的模型，都存在以下问题：由于ML模型较依赖人工，且学术界在图像特征、ML分类技术的最优组合等方面尚未达成共识，该类模型存在效率较低、主观性较高、容易忽略潜在特征等问题 [ 6 ] 。DL算法的出现将图像特征提取过程自主化，这虽然削弱了模型对人工的依赖，但也无法避免地带来的透明度低、特征解释正确性不明等问题。因此，如何权衡训练数据集、ROI的大小与训练算法的时间成本，如何在性能更佳的DL方法和可解释性更强、成本更低的传统ML方法中进行选择；为防止发生过度拟合如何识别并去除冗余特征，且不降低诊断效能等问题都是亟待解决的难题。 除此之外，在实际临床工作中，除了超声图像表现，超声医师还要综合患者的病史及其他检查结果才能作出最合理的诊断建议 [ 20 ] 。为了突破当下的研究瓶颈，还应该开发结合其他相关非影像学信息的超声AI模型。 3.2. 实验设计标准化与研究成果推广 乳腺超声AI相关研究虽多，部分产品已获得FDA批准上市，但能真正推广应用的成果却凤毛麟角。这不仅与建模技术本身的差异化有关，还与实验流程的科学性和异质性有关。因为医学影像数据的特殊性，已有研究大多都是小样本、单中心、回顾性的，国内外目前都缺乏可共享的结构化标准数据库。部分模型研究未严格设定3个独立的训练集、验证集和测试集，也因此造成了偏倚和过拟合。特征提取的方法或系统缺乏标准，也会导致实验的可重复性差，不同科研团队难以对同一特征或系统展开研究。另外，AI性能评价指标不统一，测试及验证方式也缺乏规范。 3.3. 伦理争议的困境 如同其他医疗AI技术，乳腺超声AI也面临着许多法律、道德难题。首先，无论是构建AI诊断预测系统还是提升其性能，都对大量、全面的数据库有着高度依赖。有效防止患者隐私泄露，是实现医疗数据共享之前必须解决的问题。其次，即使在超声AI的辅助下也难以完全避免误诊与漏诊，由此产生的过失如何界定责任，还需要政府部门不断完善相应的法律法规。此外，AI的介入定将改变传统医患关系，超声医师必须适应新的角色定位，维护与患者之间的良好互动。 4. 结 语 回顾超声AI诊断技术近些年的蓬勃发展，虽然新技术的出现不可避免地伴随着许多争议和挑战，但不可否定的是身处大数据时代，研发乳腺超声AI是顺应时代潮流的必然选择，更是一条解决当下我国乳腺癌严峻形势的新出路。为了早日实现超声AI诊断技术投入临床使用，学者们要立足于当下已有的理论、技术和资源基础，着眼于数据库共享、算法模型优化、特征提取标准化等方面，不断凝聚共识、寻求突破，方能朝着这个最终目标不断迈进。 基金资助 成都市科技项目(2017-CY02-00027-GX)。 This work was supported by the Chengdu Science and Technology Project (2017-CY02-00027-GX), China. 利益冲突声明 作者声称无任何利益冲突。 作者贡献 兰梓涵 文献检索与整理，论文、撰写与修改；彭玉兰 论文指导。所有作者阅读并同意最终的文本。 原文网址 http://xbyxb.csu.edu.cn/xbwk/fileup/PDF/2022081009.pdf 参考文献 1. Sung H, Ferlay J, Siegel RL, et al. Global Cancer Statistics 2020: GLOBOCAN Estimates of Incidence and Mortality Worldwide for 36 Cancers in 185 Countries. [J]. CA Cancer J Clin, 2021, 71(3): 209-249. 10.3322/caac.21660 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. 张保宁, 陈万青, 张希, 等. 中国乳腺癌防控形势面临挑战[J]. 中华肿瘤杂志, 2016, 38(10): 798-800. 10.3760/cma.j.issn.0253-3766.2016.10.018. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]; ZHANG Baoning, CHEN Wangqing, ZHANG Xi, et al. China faces a challenge of breast cancer prevention and control[J]. Chinese Journal of Oncology, 2016, 38(10): 798-800. 10.3760/cma.j.issn.0253-3766.2016.10.018. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Guo RR, Lu GL, Qin BJ, et al. Ultrasound imaging technologies for breast cancer detection and management: a review[J]. Ultrasound Med Biol, 2018, 44(1): 37-70. 10.1016/j.ultrasmedbio.2017.09.012. [ DOI ] [ PMC free ar