ar S, et al. Radiomics in Oncology: A Practical Guide. Radiographics. 2021;41(6):1717–1732. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Lubner MG. Radiomics and Artificial Intelligence for Renal Mass Characterization. Radiol Clin North Am. 2020;58(5):995–1008. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Vujanic GM, Gessler M, Ooms A, et al. The UMBRELLA SIOP-RTSG 2016 Wilms tumour pathology and molecular biology protocol. Nat Rev Urol. 2018;15(11):693–701. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. Owens CM, Veys PA, Pritchard J, Levitt G, Imeson J, Dicks-Mireaux C. Role of chest computed tomography at diagnosis in the management of Wilms'' tumor: a study by the United Kingdom Children''s Cancer Study Group. J Clin Oncol. 2002;20(12):2768–2773. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',5,'2025-10-15 10:14:52','2025-10-15 10:14:52');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4812,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10658401','Introduction to interventional radiology''s role in palliative care for children with cancer: A COG Diagnostic Imaging Committee/SPR Oncology Committee White Paper.','Hawkins CM, Gill AE.','2023','cryoneurolysis cryoneurolysis indwelling neurolytic cryoneurolysis cibp cibp cibp cibp afferents osteoclasts efficaciously cibp cibp hifu vertebroplasty kyphoplasty geometries osteoinductive humerus cryoprobe intraprocedural cryoprobe callstrom prologo hemothorax thanos bpi thoracolumbar hifu hifu coagulative hifu hifu hifu bpi cryoneurolysis cryoneurolysis unreachable cryoprobes injures wallerian regenerate epineurium perineurium cryoneurolysis cryoneurolysis inguinodynia pudendal cryoneurolysis cryoneurolysis cryoneurolysis prologo cryoneurolysis cryoneurolysis neurolysis cardioneurological cryoneurolysis cryoneurolysis splanchnic filippiadis splanchnic cryoneurolysis splanchnic splanchnic cryoneurolysis intraabdominal splanchnic cryoneurolysis lymphproliferative cryoprobes endplate t12 anterolateral cryoneurolysis postprocedural cryoneurolysis thoracenteses paracenteses loculation pleurodesis pleurodesis loculations indwelling itcs itcs pleurx tenckhoff indwelling thoracenteses fluoroscopically aeration indwelling indwelling capped canisters itcs itc pleurodesis itc itcs itc itcs itc itcs itc itcs itcs itcs itcs cryoneurolysis itc tighter u10ca180886 cibp hifu bpi itc indwelling heinze s829 dewitt rost clercq elger wangmo hammel anghelescu pankayatselvan urch 0269216304pm887ra mercadante mcwilliams tomasian hillen mercadante arcuri s0305 nickman boney meuser pietruck radbruch stute grond tomasian lacey timms osteoprotegerin osteoclast jsy bezjak s0360 kurup callstrom oligometastatic tvir dalili bazzocchi cazzato grasso kurup prologo vikingstad ridder glisson periarticular nojima devitalization revitalization pedicle cryobiology cryobiol callstrom dupuy prologo passalacqua bohnert palussiere pellerin guignard descat cornelis dixmerias diii thanos mylona galani osteocool opus deib deldar cementoplasty a5680 deib deldar cementoplasty extraspinal ghanouni kanaev dju082 dju082 roebuck farran saliken cryoneuroablation s0885 botsa poulou koutsogiannis errani longhi mavrogenis vottis mavrogenis palmerini buon bittman newsome cryoneurolysis bittman behbahani prologo cryoneurolysis wallerian prologo gilliland amputees yarmohammadi nakamoto hayek haaga filippiadis ptohis efthymiou kelekis cryoneurolysis splanchnic s00270 novy engle splanchnic neurolysis reyad othman neurolysis splanchnic ejp indwelling tunnelled kalimi khodadadian pleur weissleder gervais s00270 abrantes furtado vaz assuncao auger indwelling pleurx','Abstract As palliative care continues to be an area of increasing emphasis in pediatric oncology programs, it is important to raise awareness about minimally-invasive, image-guided procedures that can supplement more conventional palliative interventions, such as systemic analgesics, external beam radiation, and locoregional anesthesia. These procedures, when performed for appropriately selected patients, can often facilitate discharge from an inpatient facility, and help patients meet their end-of-life goals. This article specifically discusses three palliative procedures performed by interventional radiologists that can assist pediatric palliative care teams: 1) percutaneous thermal ablation of painful bone metastases, 2) cryoneurolysis, and 3) tunneled drainage catheter placement for malignant pleural effusions and ascites. Keywords: Palliative care, pediatric interventional radiology, bone tumor ablation, cryoneurolysis, indwelling tunneled drainage catheters Introduction In recent years, palliative care has become an area of increasing emphasis in pediatric oncology programs. 1 - 3 As such, children with life-threatening oncologic diseases have started to gain access to palliative interventions similar to what has been available to adults. 4 Conventional palliative interventions include systemic analgesics, external beam radiation, chemotherapy, and targeted locoregional anesthesia including intrathecal pumps, epidural injections, and focal nerve blocks with neurolytic medications such as phenol. 5 - 9 In addition to these traditional palliative treatments, modern minimally-invasive, image-guided procedures offer pediatric oncology patients additional palliative interventions that have only been available to adults up until recently. While pediatric interventional radiologists (IRs) can supplement pediatric palliative care programs in a number of ways, the purpose of this article is to describe three specific ways in which IR can aide in palliation of children with advanced cancer: 1) percutaneous thermal ablation of painful bone metastases, 2) cryoneurolysis, and 3) tunneled drainage catheter placement for malignant pleural effusions and ascites. Palliative Thermal Ablation of Painful Bone Metastases Cancer-induced bone pain (CIBP) is traditionally described as a triad of components, including movement-induced pain, background pain, and spontaneous pain. 6 , 7 , 10 Bisphosphonates and external beam radiation have both been used for treating CIBP for quite some time. 5 , 6 , 11 However, despite these interventions, it is estimated that up to 45-75% of patients with CIBP have inadequate pain management. 6 , 12 , 13 While a detailed description of the mechanisms that induce CIBP is beyond the scope of this manuscript, it can be briefly summarized as destruction of the primary bony afferents that are stimulated (and ultimately destroyed) by a number of factors excreted by tumor cells, including prostaglandins and cytokines. 6 Furthermore, local acidosis from osseous tumor invasion induces osteoclasts and increases risk for pathologic fracture. 14 While external beam radiation has been used efficaciously for CIBP, pain relief from this modality is sometimes temporary and take days to weeks to occur. 15 , 16 Additionally, more invasive locoregional anesthetic interventions, such as intrathecal pumps, may preclude patients from leaving the hospital at the end of life. As such, percutaneous thermal ablation may serve as an adjuvant palliative treatment option for pediatric patients with CIBP refractory to conventional therapy. Percutaneous thermal ablation technologies include radiofrequency ablation (RFA), cryoablation, microwave (MW) ablation, and high-intensity focused ultrasound (HIFU). The mechanisms for each ablative technology have been described extensively elsewhere, and will not be further detailed here. 13 , 16 All of the ablation technologies are aided by imaging guidance (most commonly CT and ultrasound) for thermal probe placement, and in children, are performed under general anesthesia. MR compatible cryoablation systems are available, as well, but not commonly available at pediatric hospitals. RFA is the most common ablative modality used to treat painful bone metastases in the adult population. This is likely due to the high frequency of vertebral body metastases with pathologic compression fractures that are commonly treated via RFA and concomitant vertebroplasty or kyphoplasty in adults with lung, prostate, and breast cancer. 8 , 9 , 17 , 18 However, vertebral compression fractures in pediatric patients with painful bony metastases are much less common, and is likely due to the different types of cancer and cancer biology present in children. In the authors’ experience, painful bone metastases in pediatrics and adolescents are most commonly due to metastatic sarcoma, including osteosarcoma, Ewing sarcoma, and rhabdomyosarcoma. While heat-based ablation modalities (RFA and MW ablation) have been shown to be efficacious for treating painful bone metastases in adults (reviewed below), cryoablation is the authors’ preferred ablation modality for painful bone metastases in children. ( Figure 1 ) Cryoablation has a number of advantages as compared to the heat-based modalities. These include: 1) the ability to shape the ablation zone for tumors with complex geometries (such as rib lesions), 2) the ability to visualize the ice-ball/ablation zone during the ablation, and 3) less intra and post-procedural pain compared to heat-based modalities based on the natural anesthetic effects freezing nerves. 13 Furthermore, when cryoablation is used, motor-evoked potential can be monitored for lesions that are close to critical motor nerves to avoid thermal nerve injury. 19 Even if there is thermal nerve injury during cryoablation, this has been shown to be a transient phenomenon with distal regrowth of motor nerves following freeze injury. 20 Lastly, there is emerging evidence that cryoablation provides an excellent scaffold for new bone growth, osteoinductive properties for normal bone regrowth, and an optimal safety profile for surrounding cartilage, ligaments, and tendons. 21 , 22 Figure 1: Ultrasound guided cryoablation of a painful bone metastasis. Open in a new tab 15 year old female with osteosarcoma (humerus primary site) with a painful bone metastasis. Figure 1a shows a T2-bright bone lesion (red arrow) with cortical destruction, consistent with a bone metastasis, correlating with a site of severe pain. Figure 1b shows the lesion under ultrasound, with placement of a cryoprobe (red arrows) through the lesion. Figure 1c shows the intraprocedural appearance of the ice-ball (red, dashed arrows) covering the lesion with the proximal portion of the cryoprobe still visible (red solid arrows). Criteria for percutaneous thermal ablation are typically very patient-specific. Patient age, mobility status, and bowel/bladder continence all influence if and where (anatomically) thermal ablation can be performed. However, in general, palliative thermal ablation of bony metastases should be considered when lesions are ≤8 cm and more than 0.5 – 1 cm away from the spinal cord, brain, and motor nerves. 18 Special care must be taken if ablation is performed in a weight-bearing bone, as these locations have the greatest risk of post-ablation fracture. 18 Also, eligibility for general anesthesia must be considered, as nearly all thermal ablation procedures in children are performed under anesthesia. Cryoablation Callstrom et al reported cryoablation of 69 painful bony tumors in 61 adult patients and demonstrated statistically significant decrease in the mean worst pain score at 1, 4, 8, and 24 weeks post-ablation. 23 Furthermore, 39 of 47 patients requiring opioid analgesics prior to ablation reported a reduction in dose following cryoablation. Lastly, the authors reported a major complication rate of 2%, with one case of osteomyelitis at the ablation site. 23 Prologo and colleagues analyzed cryoablation of 54 painful bone metastases in 50 patients and showed statistically significant reduction in opioid usage and Visual Analogue Scale (VAS) scores at 24 hours and 3 months. 24 Finally, an additional retrospective review of 78 painful bone tumors in adults treated via percutaneous cryoablation demonstrated a statistically significant pain score reduction at 1 day, 1 week, 1 month, and 3 months. At 6 months, the mean pain score had increased to just below pre-ablation scores. 8 The authors reported a 4.3% complication rate including 2 cases of hemothorax and 1 case of transient foot drop. RFA and MW ablation The existing literature also supports the efficacy of heat-based ablation modalities for palliation of painful bony metastatic disease. 25 Thanos et al reported their experience treating 34 bone metastases in 30 patients and showed a significant decrease brief pain inventory (BPI) scores 4 and 8 weeks after treatment with a concomitant marked reduction in analgesic needs of patients. 26 A prospective trial of 100 adult patients with thoracolumbar and/or pelvic/sacral bone metastases showed statistically significant pain reduction and quality of life when treated via RFA up through 6 months following the procedure. 27 Khan et al reported their experience using MW ablation to treat 102 spinal metastases in 69 patients and demonstrated statistically significant VAS reduction at 2-4 weeks and 20-24 weeks following the procedure. 28 Similarly, another retrospective review of 65 patients with 77 painful bone tumors (including some extra-spinal/pelvic metastases) who were treated via percutaneous MW ablation showed statistically significant reduction in VAS at 24 hours, 2-4 weeks, and 20-24 weeks following the procedure. 29 HIFU HIFU is an intriguing therapeutic option for painful bone metastases as it is entirely non-invasive, and instead uses a focused ultrasound beam (commonly with MRI guidance) to produce thermal energy and cause coagulative necrosis of targeted tissue. 13 HIFU, however, is currently limited by scarce availability and long procedural times. Early literature is encouraging though. A phase-III trial of MR-guided HIFU versus placebo for treatment of painful bony metastatic disease enrolled 137 patients and demonstrated HIFU to be superior than placebo at 3 months in numerical pain rating scale and BPI. 30 Summary Although the literature regarding palliative percutaneous thermal ablation of painful bone metastases is very limited in children 31 , 32 , the literature from experience in adults suggests that it is an efficacious palliative treatment in these challenging clinical scenarios. 33 - 36 Percutaneous ablation should be considered when traditional palliative measures are inadequate. Moving forward, it is important for the pediatric oncology and pediatric interventional radiology communities to study what tumor types, tumor locations, and tumor sizes respond most favorably to ablation, as well as the durability of those treatments. Studies such as these will improve patient selection, procedural safety, and efficacy. Cryoneurolysis Cryoneurolysis is a minimally-invasive, image-guided technique aimed at intentionally freezing nerves to relieve pain. 37 , 38 More recently, the availability of cross-sectional imaging guidance has improved the safety and ability of interventional radiologists to reach previously unreachable nerves with cryoprobes. Cryoablation injures nerves via ice-crystal formation that leads to cessation of conduction, Wallerian degeneration, and reliable, predictable regeneration. 38 This ability to regenerate following cryoablation is thought to be due to cryoablation’s ability to preserve the acellular epineurium/perineurium which provides a scaffold for subsequent regeneration. 13 , 20 , 24 , 39 Cryoneurolysis has a number of applications for both neoplastic and non-neoplastic diseases. 37 In pediatrics, the literature regarding its use is scarce. However, in adults, cryoneurolysis has been used to treat post-thoracotomy pain, lumbar facet joint syndrome, chronic pelvic/perineal pain, occipital neuralgia, inguinodynia, and pudendal neuralgia. 37 There are, however, a handful of potential efficacious palliative applications of cryoneurolysis in pediatric patients with advanced cancer, including patients with phantom limb pain and abdominal pain from disseminated cancer. Multiple studies have been performed evaluating the efficacy of cryoneurolysis in the setting of refractory phantom limb pain. As many pediatric patients suffering from sarcomatous cancers result in amputation for local control of the primary tumor, this application of cryoneurolysis may be particularly beneficial for this patient population. Prologo et al prospectively evaluated 21 patients with refractory phantom limb pain that were treated with cryoneurolysis. The authors showed statistically significant reductions in both disability scores and pain scores at long-term follow-up. 40 Specifically, disability scores were reduced from a mean of 11.3 prior to treatment to 3.3 at 45-day follow-up. VAS scores prior to the procedure were 6.6 and were reduced to 2.0 at long term follow-up (mean = 194 days). An additional potentially efficacious application of cryoneurolysis is the treatment of patients with abdominal pain in the setting of disseminated malignancy. Regarding abdominal pain, neurolysis of the celiac plexus with alcohol or phenol has been a long-standing treatment option, most commonly in patients with pancreatic cancer. However, this treatment does pose the risk of non-target distribution, diarrhea, and cardioneurological dysfunction. Furthermore, in the setting of very bulky tumors, the distribution of the medication may be limited, hindering its efficacy. While cryoneurolysis of the celiac plexus is a technically feasible procedure 41 , more recent evidence suggests that percutaneous image-guided cryoneurolysis of the splanchnic nerves may be a safer and equally efficacious therapeutic option for patients with abdominal pain resulting from cancer.( Figure 2 ) Recently, Filippiadis et al reported their experience with splanchnic nerve cryoneurolysis in 5 adult patients with pancreatic cancer and refractory abdominal pain. 42 All 5 patients reported statistically significant reductions in VAS at 1, 3, and 6 months following the procedure, in addition to reduction in analgesic usage. 42 Multiple additional studies targeting the splanchnic nerves, rather than the celiac plexus, further support splanchnic nerve cryoneurolysis for treatment of refractory abdominal pain in the setting of intraabdominal cancer. 43 , 44 Figure 2: CT-guided percutaneous splanchnic nerve cryoneurolysis. Open in a new tab 16 year old female with refractory abdominal pain in the setting of post-transplant lymphproliferative disorder. Figure 2 shows a single axial CT image following CT-guided placement of bilateral cryoprobes at the superior endplate of T12 along the anterolateral aspect of the vertebral bodies. The patient had immediate cessation of abdominal pain following the procedure. Moving forward, it is likely that cryoneurolysis will continue to be applied to a number of cancer-related pain syndromes. It should remain a consideration in pediatric patients with refractory cancer-related pain who are eligible for general anesthesia. Pediatric IRs, oncologists, and palliative care physicians should be sure to thoroughly record pre-and postprocedural pain scores and analgesic requirements, as well as cancer types and tumor sizes in order to improve efficacy of cryoneurolysis. Palliative Management of Malignant Pleural Effusions and Ascites Malignant effusions, which include both pleural effusions and ascites, can occur at time of presentation in patients with cancer, or as sequelae of disease progression. While serial thoracenteses and paracenteses are a therapeutic option for these patients, this process becomes increasing challenging due to worsening fluid loculation, and it also requires the patient to undergo multiple, continuous procedures in a hospital or office-based setting. Pleurodesis is another therapeutic option for malignant pleural effusions. However, pleurodesis is associated with prolonged hospitalization, severe pain, and worsening fluid loculations. 45 - 47 Indwelling, tunneled, cuffed, drainage catheters (ITCs), instead, offer patients a long-term option for at-home palliative management of their malignant effusions in both adults and children. 46 , 48 - 50 These catheters, in children, are placed with imaging guidance, and can be placed in both the inpatient and outpatient settings under conscious sedation or general anesthesia. ( Figure 3 ) The most commonly used ITCs are PleurX (BD, Franklin Lakes, NJ) catheters ( Figure 4 ) and Tenckhoff catheters (Medtronic, Dublin, Ireland), although the latter is more specifically designed for peritoneal dialysis rather than peritoneal drainage. Figure 3: Image-guided placement of a pleural indwelling, tunneled, cuffed, drainage catheter. Open in a new tab 12 year old male with diffusely metastatic osteosarcoma with large, persistent left pleural effusion despite multiple thoracenteses. Figure 3a shows ultrasound-guided needle access (red arrow) into the pleural effusion. Figure 3b shows fluoroscopically guided wire placement (red arrows) into the posterior pleural space to facilitate fluid drainage. Figure 3c shows the drainage catheter with the red arrows marking the multiple side-holes of the drainage catheter throughout its course. Figure 3d shows improved aeration of the left lung following evacuation of the pleural effusion with improved visualization of a large mass in the left lung (red arrow) causing the effusion. Figure 4: Pleural indwelling, tunneled, cuffed drainage catheter. Open in a new tab Figure 4a shows an indwelling, tunneled, cuffed drainage catheter designed for pleural drainage with multiple side-holes along the internalized portion of the catheter. The catheter remains capped, as shown, but is connected to vacuum suction canisters ( Figure 3b ), which the patient is able to do at home for routine, daily pleural drainage. One retrospective review of 12 ITCs in 8 pediatric patients from a single institution. They demonstrated a median time to discharge of 2 days following ITC placement. All patients were discharged with the catheters in place. The authors reported 2 major complications (pain, site infection), and drainage cessation (due to pleurodesis from the ITC or tumor progression) in 4 patients. 45 Six ITCs in five patients were in situ at the time of death. 45 An additional retrospective review of 9 pediatric and young adult patients treated with ITC for malignant effusions. 7 patients had pleural ITCs, 1 patient had a peritoneal ITC, and one patient had both pleural and peritoneal ITCs placed. Six patients were able to be discharged with catheters in place. One peritoneal ITC was removed 52 days after placement, but prior to discharge. Two patients died in the hospital with the ITCs still in place. The authors reported no catheter-related complications. 51 Comparatively, the adult literature examining the efficacy and safety of ITCs for malignant effusions in adults is more robust, but further supports ITCs as an efficacious and safe palliative option for these patients that facilitates discharge from the inpatient setting in appropriate clinical scenarios. 46 - 50 When pediatric oncologists and palliative care teams have patients with malignant effusions, either pleural or peritoneal, it is important to remember that interventional radiologists may be able to place ITCs to help facilitate discharge home for these children. Conclusion Interventional radiologists perform procedures supplement pediatric palliative care programs in a number of ways. Percutaneous thermal ablation of painful bone metastases, cryoneurolysis, and ITC placement are three such procedures. While the literature for these palliative care interventions is limited in pediatrics, the existing literature from the adult experience suggests that these procedures, when performed in the appropriate patients, can be efficacious. It is important, moving forward, for the IR and pediatric oncology communities to continue to raise awareness about these palliative procedures and measure clinical outcomes when they are performed. This can be achieved by prioritizing education about palliative interventions at both IR and pediatric oncology meetings (both national and international), achieving tighter integration between IR and palliative care programs at pediatric hospitals, and conducting multi-center, outcomes-focused studies. Funding: This manuscript was funded in part by the National Clinical Trials Network Operations Cent.er Grant U10CA180886. The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the National Institutes of Health. Abbreviations: IR Interventional radiology CIBP Cancer-induced bone pain RFA Radiofrequency ablation MW Microwave HIFU High-intensity focused ultrasound VAS Visual analog scale BPI Brief pain inventory ITC Indwelling tunneled drainage catheter Refe