INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4816,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9815055','The Society of Abdominal Radiology at 10 years: Reflections, status report, and look to the future.','Maturen KE, Canon CL, Fletcher JG, Hara AK, Kim DH, Kruskal JB, Miller FH, Remer EM, Silverman SG.','2023','sgr uroradiology intersociety uroradiology celebrated commemorate sgr uroradiology luminaries margulis marshak joachim burhenne seaman sgr acclaimed uroradiologists visionaries urography sgr bylaws sgr emeritus fsar fsur fsgr bylaws alphabetically abdominopelvic abdominopelvic abdominopelvic predecessors approachable newcomers buttressed symbiotic recognitions bestowing acclaimed medal medal igor laufer marshak bestowed treasurer treasurer liaises aarc dfp pdar sarmo bylaws bylaws subspecialize dfp dfps dfp dfps dfp etcs etcs etcs dfp dfps etcs dfp steadfastly honorees liaisons veritas dundee parlayed furnished fertilized friendships chilean solicitation hinges pdar pdar sgr generously supervises fundraising sizeable abdominalradiology uroradiology desiree veritas carrie cae maturen kmaturen umich cheri ccanon uabmc dkim uwhealth jkruskal bidmc erick remer remere1 sgsilverman bwh uroradiology abdominalradiology silvermsginaugural hhsearchterms 22silverman celebrate megibow sadowski korngold sadowski honorees balthazar sekhar magudia magudia sugi balthazar magudia sugi balthazar donelan maturen scard caserta magudia','Abstract In 2012, the Society of Abdominal Radiology (SAR) was formed by the merger of the Society of Gastrointestinal Radiologists (SGR) and the Society of Uroradiology (SUR). On the occasion of SAR’s ten year anniversary, this commentary describes important changes in society structure, the growth and diversity of society membership, new educational and research initiatives, intersociety and international outreach, and plans for the future. Keywords: Gastrointestinal radiology, Uroradiology, Abdominal radiology, Radiology societies, Mentorship Introduction In 2022, the Society of Abdominal Radiology (SAR) celebrated its 10-year anniversary, an early but significant milestone. In this perspective, we commemorate the anniversary with a brief review of the Society’s history, values, evolution, major programs, significant accomplishments, and plans for enriching the subspecialty and serving its members in the future. Society history and overview The Society of Abdominal Radiology: a union of two premier organizations The SAR was created in 2012 by the merger of the Society of Gastrointestinal Radiologists (SGR), and the Society of Uroradiology (SUR) [ 1 ]. Gastrointestinal radiology luminaries Alexander R. Margulis, MD, Richard H. Marshak, MD, H. Joachim Burhenne, MD, William B. Seaman, MD, and Walter M. Whitehouse, MD established the SGR in 1971. The SUR was formed in 1974, several years after acclaimed uroradiologists Howard M. Pollack, MD and Joshua A. Becker, MD had created a mail interchange club for urologists and radiologists with a specific interest in the urinary tract [ 2 ]. These visionaries saw the need for radiologists to sub-specialize and focus on organ systems, a concept that was relatively new to radiology. The two societies developed their common missions in clinical care, research, and education in parallel, and each was highly successful for several decades. Each society grew around the principal abdominal imaging technologies of the time: barium-based fluoroscopy for the alimentary tract and intravenous urography for the urinary tract. However, these organ-specific radiographic modalities were gradually supplemented by cross-sectional imaging technologies including ultrasound, CT, MRI, and later PET. These techniques imaged whole-body regions and included both gastrointestinal (GI) and genitourinary (GU) organs in the same exam, and often in the same image. As a result, GI and GU radiology exams were no longer separate, and radiology divisions previously known as ‘GI’, ‘GU’, and ‘Body’ (some of which had included all organs outside the head and neck), were gradually replaced with ‘Abdominal’ divisions. In the mid-1990s, SUR and SGR leaders began merger discussions [ 1 ]. In 2000, the first joint scientific meeting of the two societies was held, although their business and operational affairs remained separate. In 2003, an Abdominal Radiology Consortium was formed, including members of the Boards of Directors of both societies. In 2007, the societies agreed to fully merge. Over the subsequent five years, both Boards worked together to create the vision and bylaws for the new Society. The overarching rationale was that a large, unified society would mirror abdominal radiology practice, include both diagnostic imaging and interventional radiology (IR), better serve its members, and be a stronger and more influential body. In 2012, the formation of the SAR was official, and the inaugural meeting was held in 2013. Society membership and growth Prior to the merger, the SUR and SGR each had fewer than 400 members. As of fall 2022, the SAR had grown to over 1700 members, including emeritus and international members, and more than 450 members-in-training. The designation Fellow of the SAR (FSAR) recognizes service and contribution to the society and subspecialty and has replaced the FSUR and FSGR designations. More than 300 members are currently Fellows. Society mission, values, and goals With the creation of the SAR in 2012, the original bylaws included multiple goals that each focused on abdominal radiology and included fostering advances in diagnosis and intervention, providing a forum for the exchange of knowledge pertaining to research, practice, and education, stimulating investigation and teaching in the methods of preventing and treating disease, and providing continuing medical education for its members. In 2016 and later in 2022, the Board of Directors revised the goals to provide a broader, more encompassing set of statements that included the Society’s mission (which also expressed an overall vision), values (newly expressed as single terms and listed alphabetically to express equal importance), as well as its goals (Table 1 ). The repeated re-assessment of the Society’s goals has been intentional, to incorporate new ideas and adapt to advances in imaging and image-guided intervention, our understanding of pathophysiology, and new treatment options. These updated statements clarify the central tenets of the SAR, and help guide and anchor decision making and prioritization in a dynamic environment. The goals were designed to be specific and actionable. The statements allow for the more concrete goals to change over time, while the mission and values stay constant. The updated mission, vision, and values statements highlight the SAR’s emphasis on scientific rigor and educational excellence. Table 1. Society of Abdominal Radiology Mission, Values and Goals Mission To promote patient health through scientific discovery, innovation, education, collaboration, and leadership development in abdominopelvic radiology Values Clinical Excellence Collaboration Compassion Inclusion Integrity Mentorship Respect Scientific Expertise Service Goals Create transformative educational content for all levels of learners Support abdominopelvic radiology research and information exchange Promote interdisciplinary and international collaborations Mentor and develop the abdominopelvic radiologists and SAR leaders of the future Ensure an inclusive and transparent society Open in a new tab Society culture of mentorship The SAR has maintained the longstanding tradition of its predecessors of providing an approachable, and friendly culture (both at and between annual meetings), where newcomers are welcomed, and members are supported at every stage of their careers. Despite substantial society growth, informal mentorship opportunities at the annual meeting continue and are buttressed further by new, formal mentorship programs that are embedded in many of the Society’s programs described below. Society journal: Abdominal Radiology Soon after the merger, the subspecialty journal, Abdominal Imaging, became the official journal of the SAR [ 1 ]. This was a critical step for the Society, as it provided both a written and online medium for publishing the latest scientific research, educational materials, and highlights of the annual meeting. In 2016, the journal’s name was changed to Abdominal Radiology to acknowledge the inclusion of IR in the subspecialty; the new name had the additional benefit of matching the Society’s name [ 3 ]. The relationship between the Society and the Journal has been symbiotic; while society members have benefited, the Journal has grown in both number of publications and impact factor [ 4 ]. Society recognitions of excellence The SAR has continued its rich traditions of recognizing members who have contributed to abdominal radiology by bestowing research, scientific, and recognition awards. Among several awards given by the SAR each year, the most highly acclaimed, the Howard M. Pollack MD, and Walter B. Cannon MD Awards, named to honor pioneers in GU and GI radiology respectively, were awarded until 2019, when they were combined with the pre-existing GU and GI Lifetime Achievement Awards into a single, SAR Gold Medal Award. The Award features profiles of Drs. Pollack and Cannon on each side of the medal. Other distinctive annual awards include the Igor Laufer Visiting Professorship and the Richard Marshak International Lecturer awards, both bestowed on some of the Society’s best educators, and numerous additional annual meeting awards for research and education. Key changes, developments, and new directions Society governance redesign The original SAR Board of Directors consisted of six members, a President, a President-Elect, a Secretary-Treasurer, two Directors-in-Succession, and an Immediate Past President. Board members rotated roles each year. In 2016, concurrent with a major strategic planning initiative, the roles of the Directors-in-Succession and Secretary-Treasurer were changed to three year terms to ensure leadership continuity. Each Director had oversight of a key area: Science, Education, and Finance. In 2017, a Director-at-Large position was added to the board, with the newest board member designated to oversee new and emerging initiatives. At the end of the initial year of service the Director-at-Large assumed the role of the Director (Science, Education, or Finance) who had just rotated into the President-Elect position. To accommodate Society growth (e.g., new and expanded programs in both research and education) and allow the Board to focus on long-term strategy while still providing operational oversight, the governance of the Society was further expanded in 2021 to create 6 new, distinct “Portfolios” (Clinical Practice Innovation, Science and Research, Lifelong Learning, Member Engagement, Meeting Education, and International and External Relations) (Fig. 1 ). Each Portfolio is led by a Portfolio Director who serves for three years and liaises with a designated Board member, who retains his/her specific operational task (Clinical Practice, Science, Education, Finance & Engagement) for four years. The Portfolio Directors assume major operational oversight as they work directly with specified committee chairs. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Organizational chart of the Society of Abdominal Radiology. Schematic of work portfolios does not include all tasks conducted by Board of Directors and Portfolio Directors; not all SAR committees are listed. AARC: Association of Abdominal Radiology Chiefs, ACR: American College of Radiology, CME: Continuing Medical Education, DEI : Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion, DFP/ETC: Disease Focused Panel/Emerging Technology Commissions, PDAR: Program Directors in Abdominal Radiology, RFS: Residents and Fellows Section, SARMO: SAR Medical Student Organization, SAR: Society of Abdominal Radiology In conjunction with the redesigned governance structure and creation of portfolios, the Rules and Bylaws committee worked with the Membership Committee to move operational details to a new Society Policy and Operations Manual, approved at the 2022 Business Meeting. These changes permit additional flexibility and speed in adapting to changes in society operations and members’ needs. Society Disease-Focused Panels and Emerging Technology Commissions To continue the parent societies’ focus on organ systems, the original SAR bylaws called for each member to join a GI or GU chapter. As medical knowledge has grown and become increasingly rich and complex, medical specialists have been driven to subspecialize in an organ system and even “super specialize” in a particular disease. The merger into a general abdominal radiology society created an umbrella organization for many highly specialized radiologists, but a structure was needed to develop specific disease expertise among its members. In response, the SAR established the Disease-Focused Panel (DFP) Program in 2013 and eliminated the GI and GU chapters [ 5 ]. DFPs focus on a disease, or a particular aspect of a disease, and work to improve patient care, research, and education. DFP products include clinical protocols, interdisciplinary consensus guidelines, teaching materials and hands-on experiences, and original, multi-institutional research that addresses key knowledge gaps. DFPs include non-radiologist physicians and other relevant specialist consultants and provide a forum to identify gaps in knowledge, evaluate evidence, and guide medical practice. The DFP Program was expanded to include Emerging Technology Commissions (ETCs) in 2018. Rather than focusing on a disease, ETCs are centered on advanced imaging and interventional technologies. ETCs work with physicists, engineers, and industrial partners to develop a shared perspective, generate research and validation methods, and disseminate new knowledge. Because of the need to collaborate with multiple scientific and industrial partners, the SAR DFP-ETC Oversight Committee developed language and processes to appropriately manage intellectual property and industrial relationships. To date, 22 DFPs and ETCs have been created and have published widely, including more than 150 articles in Abdominal Radiology alone [ 4 ]. Each DFP and ETC has a specific mentorship pathway that promotes early career development and gives young faculty valuable access to experienced mentors and sponsors beyond their home institutions. Society focus on diversity, equity, and inclusion The SAR has embraced numerous diversity, equity, and inclusion (DEI) initiatives. In 2020, a DEI Committee was established. Their vision is to create “a diverse and inclusive society where all members feel welcomed and can access opportunities for career advancement and success”, with a mission to “advocate for diversity and inclusivity and to promote accessibility to SAR resources and opportunities so that all SAR members, faculty, and trainees can advance in their careers in research, education, and service to the society.” The definition of diversity is appropriately broad and includes not only gender, race, and ethnicity, but other facets such as geography, institution, generation, experience, disability, and gender expression. Leadership choices and award decisions are steadfastly made through a lens of diversity. In addition to assuring diversity of meeting participants [ 6 ], the DEI Committee collects and reviews diversity data of SAR leadership and honorees [ 7 ]. The SAR website features diversity, equity, and inclusion content. DEI committee liaisons provide outreach to numerous other committees, to help create inclusive and equitable processes across the society. For example, annual meeting programming committees formally consider diversity when selecting plenary speakers, moderators, panel participants, and workshop presenters, and intentionally include DEI content in the program. The DEI Committee has also worked to guide committee leadership throughout the Society. In general, a Society-wide rotational model establishes 3-year term limits to promote opportunities for others who are interested and foster diversity among SAR Committee leaders. A pathway to create new ad hoc committees addresses emerging needs within the Society; these temporary committees may become permanent after a 3-year trial period and Board of Directors approval. Society expansion into year-round activities and virtual platforms In 2019, the Board retained Veritas Association Management of East Dundee, Illinois to manage the Society. This change helped the Society expand beyond the annual meeting and add online education and other activities, both during the meeting and throughout the year. Although initially necessitated by the COVID-19 pandemic, the SAR has parlayed virtual platforms to deliver high quality education year-round. The SAR 2021 meeting was entirely virtual, and the 2022 meeting hybrid; many members attended in person, and many remotely. All members had access to the 2022 meeting content online for several months after the meeting. The SAR Online Education Committee selects portions of annual meeting content to be made available to members of the Society as enduring materials on the SAR website. The SAR collaborated with the Association of University Radiologists and created an online ‘Radiology Resident Core Curriculum Lecture Series in Abdominal Radiology’. The SAR also offers numerous webinars throughout the calendar year, with educational content furnished by various committees. To complement the in-person Hands-On Courses at the annual meeting, virtual versions of some courses have been created and are offered in the fall. All these initiatives result in a Society that engages its membership throughout the year. Society efforts in international collaboration The SAR has longstanding positive relationships with international sister societies including the European Society of Urogenital Radiology, the European Society of Gastrointestinal and Abdominal Radiology, and the Asian Society of Abdominal Radiology. Beyond these, international activity has grown since 2012, catalyzed by efforts of multiple SAR Presidents, and fertilized by friendships and collaborations arising from the annual International Educational Conference (IEC) in which selected SAR lecturers present at a selected country outside the U.S. In 2019, the idea of SAR outreach to specific global regions was introduced, and SAR members met with 30 abdominal radiologists from Latin America to discuss how to engage radiologists in these countries. In 2020, an International Task Force composed of a diverse array of SAR members including past-presidents and early career radiologists was created. At the 2021 annual meeting, the first set of international plenary sessions, featuring India and Latin America, were included in the Program; some of the content was delivered in native languages. These efforts have led to SAR lecturers participating in virtual international meetings held by the Chilean Radiology Society and the Brazilian College of Radiology and Diagnostic Imaging. In 2022, the International Task Force became the permanent SAR International Relations Committee that will manage a range of issues including international membership, international trainees, oversight of topic solicitation process for virtual and in-person educational programs, relationships with international radiological societies, service, and educational resources. Society support of young members The long-term future of any organization hinges on the enthusiasm and engagement of its young members, who bring vitality, sense of purpose, and new perspectives. As such, the SAR has launched affinity groups for junior members: the Resident and Fellow Section (RFS) and the Early Career Committee (ECC) [ 8 ]. To amplify the voices of young members, representatives of the RFS and ECC have been appointed to many of the standing committees that govern and conduct the business of the SAR. Both groups have organized events to further social interaction and networking, promote wellness, orient novices to meeting navigation, provide mentorship in career advice and advancement, create learning opportunities, and promote academic inquiry. Social events, athletic activities, and speed mentoring are offered during the annual meeting, while evening webinars and other virtual events span the calendar year. A combined RFS/ECC mentorship program pairs established SAR members with early career radiologists (defined as within 7 years of beginning practice) for a one-year experience that covers topics such as career guidance, research ideas, and work/life balance. Supported by the RFS and ECC, the percentage of trainees and early career members in the SAR has increased in recent years. Society leadership development In 2021, the SAR launched two committees to support member institution leaders in abdominal radiology; two roles were targeted, Division Chiefs and Fellowship Directors. The Association of Abdominal Radiology Chiefs shares best practices on how to manage a busy academic abdominal radiology division, and how to cope with clinical service delivery at times of stress such as managing diagnostic exam volume shifts and staffing shortages related to the COVID-19 pandemic, and more recently the iodinated contrast media shortage. The Program Directors in Abdominal Radiology (PDAR) Committee leads the SAR’s approach to standardizing abdominal radiology fellowship recruitment [ 9 – 11 ] and has partnered with the Society of Chairs of Academic Radiology Departments to develop guidance documents. By establishing an abdominal radiology fellowship curriculum and hosting a series of educational webinars for fellows, the PDAR Committee is developing a national community for abdominal radiology fellow education and shaping the future of the subspecialty. Society fund for research and education Like the SUR and the SGR before it, the SAR has been supported generously by industry partners. Following the merger in 2012, the Board initiated a separate SAR Fund that focuses on research and education and is supported entirely by individual donors. The SAR Fund Oversight Committee supervises fund activities including investment strategies, fundraising initiatives, and distribution. The Board has highlighted the fund as a strategic priority, allocating a portion of net revenues to the SAR Fund annually. The long-term goal is to grow the fund to the extent where sizeable research and education grants and other activities are funded from the generated interest, without spending principal. Recognizing that several years might be needed to reach this goal, a decision was made to supplement the fund to allow meaningful distributions during the initial growth phase. As a result, the SAR Fund is now an important driver of concrete activities. For example, the Fund sponsors multiple trainee travel grants, and the Richard L. Baron, MD Award that supports a faculty member from the country that hosted the IEC to travel to the SAR annual meeting. Society information hub Additional information can be found on a newly redesigned website ( https://abdominalradiology.org ). The Website and Social Media Committees ensure that the site describes in more detail the areas described in this perspective, while providing access to educational content and periodic updates of Society activities and programs to members and visitors. Conclusion Although still relatively young, the SAR is fulfilling the original goals of the merger and growing in its size, scope, and influence. The SAR is the largest North American society devoted to the subspecialty of Abdominal Radiology. The SAR retains the rich traditions of its parent societies, enhances the mentorship of its members, and continues to host successful annual meetings during which the latest breakthroughs in abdominal radiology are reported, and educational content is provided. SAR engagement with its members is now year-round, with a multitude of educational offerings and opportunities for collaborations in research and clinical practice improvement. Over the next 10 years (and beyond) the SAR will continue to grow and adapt to new challenges, expand its influence, and remain committed to meeting the needs of its members. All physicians, allied personnel, and trainees interested in abdominal radiology who are not yet members of the society are encouraged to join.. Acknowledgements The authors thank Society of Uroradiology past president James Ellis, MD, for his suggestion to prepare this anniversary manuscript, Society of Abdominal Radiology past president Desiree Morgan, MD for her insights and suggestions, Veritas Association Management--particularly Carrie O’Connor, CAE--for assistance with Society of Abdominal Radiology facts and figures, and Vanessa Allen of the University of Michigan Media Group for preparing the Figure. Declarations Conflict of interest There are no financial conflicts of interest. All of the authors are current or former members of the SAR board, serving as volunteers. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Contributor Information Katherine E. Maturen, Email: kmaturen@umich.edu Cheri L. Canon, Email: ccanon@uabmc.edu Joel G. Fletcher, Email: Fletcher.Joel@mayo.edu Amy K. Hara, Email: Hara.Amy@mayo.edu David H. Kim, Email: DKim@uwhealth.org Jonathan B. Kruskal, Email: jkruskal@bidmc.harvard.edu Frank H. Miller, Email: Frank.Miller@nm.org Erick M. Remer, Email: REMERE1@ccf.org Stuart G. Silverman, Email: sgsilverman@bwh.harvard.edu References 1. Silverman SG. Society of Abdominal Radiology is created by a merger of the Society of Gastrointestinal Radiologists and the Society of Uroradiology: Form follows function. Radiology. 2012;265:336–338. doi: 10.1148/radiol.12120962. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Silverman SG. A Message from the SAR Inaugural President. Accessed June 18, 2022. https://abdominalradiology.site-ym.com/page/SilverMsgInaugural?&hhsearchterms=%22silverman%22 . 3. Johnson CD. Abdominal Radiology: A new name for an evolutionary change. Abdom Radiol . 2016;41(1) 4. Johnson CD. Abdominal Radiology and SAR a decade of collaboration to celebrate! Abdom Radiol (NY). 2022;47(6):1911. doi: 10.1007/s00261-022-03527-z. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Silverman SG, Megibow AJ, Fletcher JG. Society of Abdominal Radiology Disease-Focused Panel Program: rationale for its genesis and status report. Abdom Radiol (NY). 2017;42(8):2033–2036. doi: 10.1007/s00261-017-1115-6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Shah A, Sadowski EA, Thomas K, et al. Gender and racial diversity among plenary session speakers at the Society of Abdominal Radiology Annual Meetings: a five-year assessment. Abdom Radiol (NY). 2022;47(7):2545–2551. doi: 10.1007/s00261-022-03548-8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Thomas KL, Zafar H, Parikh P, Korngold E, Canon CL, Sadowski E. Representation of women among leadership and honorees within the Society of Abdominal Radiology, past and present. Abdom Radiol (NY). 2021;46(12):5758–5762. doi: 10.1007/s00261-020-02922-8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Balthazar P, Sekhar A, Magudia K. New blood: trainee and early career engagement augmenting the society of abdominal radiology experience. Abdom Radiol (NY). 2021;46(12):5475–5479. doi: 10.1007/s00261-021-03144-2. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Magudia K, Sugi MD, Balthazar P, et al. Prospects of a Fellowship Match for Abdominal Imaging: A National Survey by the Society of Abdominal Radiology. J Am Coll Radiol. 2020;17(6):804–811. doi: 10.1016/j.jacr.2020.02.003. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Magudia K, Sugi MD, Balthazar P, Donelan K, Gupta RT, Maturen KE. The SCARD Fellowship Policy and the Abdominal Imaging Fellowship: A Follow-up Survey After the First Year. Acad Radiol . 16 2020;doi:10.1016/j.acra.2020.12.004 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 11. Gupta RT, Caserta MP, Magudia K, et al. Past, present, and future of abdominal radiology fellowship recruitment. Abdom Radiol (NY). 2021;46(12):5462–5465. doi: 10.1007/s00261-021-03257-8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',2,'2025-10-15 10:15:08','2025-10-15 10:15:08');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4817,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5775030','Tobacco papers and tobacco industry ties in regulatory toxicology and pharmacology.','Velicer C, St Helen G, Glantz SA.','2017','rtp rtp rtp rtp secondhand rtp istrp criticised istrp heckman cspi istrp rtp isrtp dow agrosciences eastman kodak gillette indspec sapphire plough smithkline cspi rtp rtp isrtp reconstitute rtp isrtp rtp isrtp cspi rtp rtp rtp ttdl industrydocuments sourcewatch rtp rtp snus teabag cspi cotinine cotinine tsna nnal inhaler tsna puffing snus cotinine snus kretek kretek kretek kretek hphcs bans snus tsna nnal kretek kretek kretek kretek kretek kretek flavours inhaler blends magnitudes snus dissolvable rtp gio gori gladys racketeer corrupt gori spokesperson luik gio gori betrayal rtp gio gori biobehavioral friedhelm korte pariza tassc baldrick covance burdock dourson tera ciar flamm gaylor ginevan ciar hirayama secondhand henschler hutt lawyer covington burling lobbied rjr maxim coauthored paustenbach quill secondhand yingling lawyer rtp hugh bolger exponent clary hoechst celanese dow corning celanese toxicos pty alcoa pinjarra alumina refinery alcoa krewski marcello lotti montedison polyvinyl lotti testified jurgen pauluhn coauthored chevron conocophillips exxonmobil rtp chisquare unaffiliated rtp rtp iqos rtp cites rtp iqos mrtp mrtp rtp nitrosonornicotine nnn smokeless deeming cigars rtp rtp rtp rtp credence velicer gideon glantz wertz kyriss paranjape glantz e1001145 garne schick glantz tob scollo hyland glantz hospitality tob oeh brokers junk publicintegrity brokers junk thacker psmag a1dbe48cca1c pmiscienceusa cspinet final_letter_academic_press_rtp cataldo prochaska glantz swaen meijers bf02602327 rochon gurwitz simms huss hug huwiler muntener roosli schillinger mangurian l16 s0273230015000835 industrydocuments sxvd0052 ligett gio batta gori industrydocuments hfvg0138 chilcote lorillard industrydocuments slxm0123 iaq lorillard industrydocuments tlxm0123 gladys rjr industrydocumentslibrary kyfj0224 industrydocuments tpnh0078 philipmorris forschungspreis philip_morris_forschungspreis friedhelm_korte friedhelm_korte pariza solana pariza industrydocuments tpbl0071 publicintegrity industrydocumentslibrary xhdp0076 sourcewatch malaspina sourcewatch alex_malaspina bts baldrick thebts bts baldrick advisorycommittees committeesmeetingmaterials tobaccoproductsscientificadvisorycommittee ucm249320 burdock invoice industrydocuments sxhf0220 burdock contractor burdock industrydocuments tjnb0219 burdock solana burdock contractor burdock industrydocuments qxmb0219 favorite publicintegrity favorite sourcewatch flamm sourcewatch w_gary_flamm flamm invoice industrydocumentslibrary ffkd0126 carchman industrydocuments fzfl0021 oldaker hirayama ginevan industrydocuments nnmm0092 henschler industrydocumentslibrary qldc0099 henschler industrydocumentslibrary qrfv0102 gruben henschler bitten herrn colby genrichteten schreiben januar umschriebene forschungsvorhaben massgabe nachstehenden regelungen zweck gesamtsumme industrydocumentslibrary hrdb0094 hutt downgrades industrydocumentslibrary txdm0100 daynard industrydocumentslibrary fjdm0100 hutt congressman bliley industrydocumentslibrary rzvc0024 covington hutt hutt maxim hereinafter rjr rjr industrydocumentslibrary hlmy0185 2srt 2srt nct00601042 joksic spasojevic tisma antic rutqvist snus reduct colucci colucci bliley colucci furr industrydocuments ffpb0024 beveridge industrydocuments rhxg0068 raul industrydocuments qzhg0068 industrydocuments xmgy0046 industrydocuments qqhg0079 rjr nabisco industrydocuments pqhg0079 tpn toxforum tpn hugh exponent bolger dabt exponent bolger toxconsult australizaia toxconsult alcoa pinjarra alumina refinery pty alcoa alumina austra caps6218 alcoa pinjarra javed cellphones thestar cellphones_and_health_panel_chair_accused_of_conflict processo montedison tumori soltanto dio sapere gazzettadimantova gelocal mantova cronaca processo montedison tumori soltanto dio sapere juergen pauluhn konference tanger idprof tragedy publicintegrity tragedy chartres scrinis s1368980016003128 mandrioli e0162198 olegario lipowicz tsna olegario lipowicz tsna nnal shepperd eldridge mcadam sakaguchi kakehi minami futamura tayyarah mrtpa tobaccoproducts marketingandadvertising ucm560044 mrtp tobaccoproducts marketingandadvertising ucm546281 nitrosonornicotine smokeless docket gpo fdsys pkg deeming docket gpo fdsys pkg bitton neuman barnoya glantz','Abstract We examined the relationship between the tobacco industry and the journal Regulatory Toxicology and Pharmacology ( RTP ) using the Truth Tobacco Industry Documents Library and internet sources. We determined the funding relationships, and categorised the conclusions of all 52 RTP papers on tobacco or nicotine between January 2013 and June 2015, as “positive”, “negative” or “neutral” for the tobacco industry. RTP ’s editor, 57% (4/7) of associate editors and 37% (14/38) of editorial board members had worked or consulted for tobacco companies. Almost all (96%, 50/52) of the papers had authors with tobacco industry ties. Seventy-six percent (38/50) of these papers drew conclusions positive for industry; none drew negative conclusions. The two papers by authors not related to the tobacco industry reached conclusions negative to the industry ( p <.001). These results call into question the confidence that members of the scientific community and tobacco product regulators worldwide can have in the conclusions of papers published in RTP . Keywords: Conflict of interest, Tobacco industry, Journal bias, Regulatory policy Introduction Tobacco companies have a long history of funding and conducting research that supports their political, regulatory and legal positions [ 1 – 5 ]. In 1989, at a time when the movement to create smoke-free indoor environments was accelerating, the tobacco industry assisted in establishing the International Society of the Built Indoor Environment that, in turn, published the journal Indoor and Built Environment . These forces established an editorial board dominated by people who had financial associations with the tobacco industry (67% in 1992 and 66% in 2002) and in which 61% of the papers on tobacco smoke in the environment (that many people call ‘secondhand smoke’) supported industry positions [ 2 ]. The journal Regulatory Toxicology and Pharmacology ( RTP ), published by Elsevier on behalf of the International Society of Regulatory Toxicology and Pharmacology (ISTRP), has been criticised for ties to major industries, including tobacco by academics, public interest groups and journalists [ 6 – 9 ]. Indeed, in June 1999 the ISTRP held its June 1999 council meeting in the Washington, D.C. offices of Keller and Heckman, a major law firm that represents the chemical industry [ 8 ]. In 2002, a public interest nutrition policy advocacy organisation, the Center for Science in the Public Interest (CSPI), sent Elsevier a letter in which 43 scientists from around the world expressed concern about the journal’s ties to ISTRP and the composition of the journal’s editorial staff, particularly conflicts of interest, lack of transparency and the absence of editorial independence [ 6 ]. Specifically, the letter stated that the journal exhibited a “bias in favor of industries that are subject to governmental health and environmental regulations and that provide support to RTP’s sponsor, ISRTP”. The letter identified the American Chemistry Council, Bristol-Myers Squibb, Dow AgroSciences, Eastman Kodak, Gillette Company, Indspec Chemical Corporation, Merck and Co., Inc., Proctor and Gamble, RJ Reynolds Tobacco Company, The Sapphire Group, Inc., Schering Plough Research Institute and SmithKline Pharmaceuticals as companies or trade associations that had a direct incentive to minimise the regulatory burden on industry. CSPI also identified 16 specific members of the RTP editorial board with ties to industries. The letter requested that Elsevier make three changes at RTP : (1) sever its ties to the industry-sponsored ISRTP, (2) reconstitute its advisory board to dramatically reduce the influence of industry scientists, industry lawyers and academic consultants to industry and (3) adopt an editorial policy of transparency about conflicts of interest [ 6 , 10 ]. In response to this letter, Elsevier improved disclosure requirements [ 10 ], but refused to make any changes to the editorial board, responding that “regulatory issues by their very nature affect industry and it is therefore only logical that editorial board members have ties to industry” and that “RTP’s relationship with the ISRTP has been and continues to be a productive one, and we have no intention of encouraging RTP to sever ties with the ISRTP” [ 6 ]. As of December, 2016, 8 of the 16 editors listed in the CSPI letter remained on the board [ 11 ]. Based on existing literature that had found tobacco [ 5 , 12 , 13 ] and other [ 14 – 19 ] industry-affiliated authors are more likely to find positive outcomes in articles evaluating tobacco, we (1) examined the tobacco affiliations of the RTP editorial board, (2) examined the author affiliation of papers on tobacco or nicotine published in RTP and (3) evaluated the conclusions of these papers as “positive”, “negative” or “neutral” with respect to the tobacco industry. Methods RTP editors and editorial board As of June 2015, the journal’s website listed the editor-in-chief, 7 associate editors and 38 editorial board members [ 20 ]. We searched the University of California San Francisco Truth (formerly Legacy) Tobacco Documents Library (TTDL: http://industrydocuments.library.ucsf.edu/tobacco ) for these 45 individuals to identify ties to the tobacco companies including full-time employment, contract work and compensation received. (“Ties” do not include indirect affiliation, such as shares in investment companies that have shares in tobacco, information that is not generally available publicly.) We also completed general web searches on these individuals to further identify ties to tobacco. And we identified relationships with industries other than the tobacco industry using general web sources, including SourceWatch.org, the Center for Science in the Public Interest, the individuals’ LinkedIn accounts, online biographies and reported research funding. Tobacco-related papers published in RTP We searched the RTP website on June 15, 2015 [ 20 ] for “tobacco” in papers published between January 2013 and June 2015. (We used this period of time because we started data collection for this project in June 2015 and wanted to include several years’ data to obtain an adequate sample size.) This search yielded 76 items; we excluded 24 from further analysis because they were not peer-reviewed papers (e.g. letters to the editor or responses) or not related to nicotine-containing products (cigarettes, ‘snus’ [a form of oral tobacco consisting of fine ground tobacco and additives in a teabag-sized pouch], electronic cigarettes, reduced harm products), policy, toxicity and/or their impact on health. We reviewed the remaining 52 papers to determine if the authors disclosed working for a tobacco company or a company consulting for the tobacco company, were funded by a tobacco company, had no obvious affiliations or where the situation was not clear. (Elsevier’s policy change on author disclosure made in response to the CSPI letter allowed us to determine these author affiliations.) We scored the conclusions of the papers as “positive”, “neutral” or “negative” for the tobacco industry using two independent reviewers, then a third to resolve any differences. We scored conclusions “positive” for industry if they supported claims of lower risk for products, for cessation benefits or challenged conclusions of public health authorities on the dangers of tobacco products; as “negative” if conclusions challenged reduced risk claims or supported conclusions of public health authorities and as “neutral” where we found no clear positive or negative implications for industry positions. Of the 52 papers, the two initial reviewers agreed on scoring for 42 (81%) of the papers. Table 1 lists the 52 papers, links to the tobacco industry and scores. Table 1. Papers Related to Tobacco or Nicotine Published in Regulatory Toxicology and Pharmacology. January 2013 through June 2015 Year Title PMID Tobacco industry link Conclusion for tobacco industry 2013 Changes in levels of biomarkers of exposure and biological effect in a controlled study of smokers switched from conventional cigarettes to reduced toxicant prototype cigarettes 23537587 Yes Positive 2013 Estimation of mouth level exposure to smoke constituents of cigarettes with different tar levels using filter analysis 24113618 Yes Positive 2013 The effect of reducing the number of cigarettes smoked on risk of lung cancer, COPD, cardiovascular disease and FEV(1)-a review 24013038 Yes Positive 2013 Aspects of the design protocol and the statistical methods for analysis of tar, nicotine and carbon monoxide yields in cigarette smoke that can affect the measurement variability within collaborative studies 23959062 Yes Positive 2013 Good relationship between saliva cotinine kinetics and plasma cotinine kinetics after smoking one cigarette 23933006 Yes Neutral 2013 A dynamic population model for estimating all-cause mortality due to lifetime exposure history 23933005 Yes Positive 2013 TSNA exposure from cigarette smoking: 18 years of urinary NNAL excretion data 23920111 Yes Positive 2013 Pharmacokinetic analysis of nicotine when using non-combustion inhaler type of tobacco product in Japanese adult male smokers 23891672 Yes Neutral 2013 How rapidly does the excess risk of lung cancer decline following quitting smoking? A quantitative review using the negative exponential model 23764305 Yes Neutral 2013 TSNA levels in machine-generated mainstream cigarette smoke: 35 years of data 23557986 Yes Positive 2013 Physical harm d