INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4824,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10785907','Optimizing the Synergistic Potential of Pseudo-Labels from Radiology Notes and Annotated Ground Truth in Identifying Pulmonary Opacities on Chest Radiographs for Early Detection of Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome.','Arora M, Davis CM, Mondal A, Gowda NR, Foster DG, Kamaleswaran R.','2024','sequela parsing noncardiogenic sequela airspace immensely sparsity tedious occluding curating annotating parsed parsed irvin medspacy medspacy sentencizer matcher negation sectionizer demarcate labeler densenet121 optimizer initialized convex adaptively emboldened bootstrapped focusses opacified opacified workups saliences calibrate parse densenet121 r01gm139967 ul1tr002378 gm14 gm095442 picks matthay brodie slutsky manoharan laffey bellani kerchberger semler janz explor e0457 sjoding courey iwashyna irvin ciurea ilcus chute aew greenbaum lungren s41597 tmh boag szolovits nabulsi sellergren jamshy kiraly heiliger sekuboyina menze kleesiek sonka maximization warfield gholipour eyre medspacy maaten kingma girshick selvaraju cogswell vedantam iccv reignier beuret gacouin boulain nejmoa1214103 nejm200005043421801 bellani laffey madotto 1306oc gajic dabbagh adesanya 0549oc tabaie eards e0257056 strous mta osch fhm vogelaar barten farchi e0258841 kamaleswaran sadan kandiah coopersmith explor e0570 s12871 henriques knowl pessach shmueli mukhoti kulharia sanyal golodetz torr dokania calibrating torrvision','Abstract Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) is a life-threatening lung injury, hallmarks of which are bilateral radiographic opacities. Studies have shown that early recognition of ARDS could reduce severity and lethal clinical sequela. A Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) model that can identify bilateral pulmonary opacities on chest x-ray (CXR) images can aid early ARDS recognition. Obtaining large datasets with ground truth labels to train CNNs is challenging, as medical image annotation requires clinical expertise and meticulous consideration. In this work, we implement a natural language processing pipeline that extracts pseudo-labels CXR images by parsing radiology notes for abnormal findings. We obtain ground-truth annotations from clinicians for the presence of pulmonary opacities for a subset of these images. A knowledge distillation-based teacher-student training framework is implemented to leverage the larger dataset with noisy pseudo-labels. Our results show an AUC of 0.93 (95%CI 0.92-0.94) for the prediction of bilateral opacities on chest radiographs. Introduction: Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) is a serious complication of trauma and critical illness. Despite extensive research, the identification and diagnosis or ARDS remains a clinical challenge and many targeted therapeutic clinical trials have failed. The identification and diagnosis of ARDS is based on several clinical criteria. In 2012, the Berlin definition of ARDS ( 1 ) defined this syndrome as the acute onset of respiratory failure caused by noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, hypoxemia, and bilateral pulmonary opacities on chest radiographs. The development of broad consensus definitions of syndromes helps guide clinicians and clinical research, however due to their broad nature, many limitations exist and should be addressed. Although there is no single diagnostic test that can rule in or rule out ARDS ( 2 ), one significant limitation both clinically and with machine learning (ML) models is with chest radiographs ( 3 – 5 ). Chest radiographs are non-specific, unreliable in nature, and interpretation of these images has significant inter-rater variability ( 6 ). Despite advancements in management, morbidity, mortality and healthcare, the costs remain high. ARDS research has shifted to early recognition and systematic evaluation of high-risk patients to reduce the severity and lethal clinical sequela. Chest x-rays are clinically valuable and can influence patient management. Interpretation of chest x-rays and understanding both negative and positive findings require expert knowledge, clinical judgement based on individualized patient care, experience, and sound decision making. There has, however, been a rise of interest in using computer vision and artificial intelligence to find CXR abnormalities. An automatic system that can raise alerts on detecting CXR findings can be of help in the early diagnoses of lung diseases and injuries. It can reduce the burden of reviewing CXR images in situations where there is a high patient-to-radiologist ratio. Specific to our problem, a system that can identify lung airspace opacities in chest x-rays of critically ill patients reliably and robustly can be immensely useful in early diagnosis of ARDS. Sparsity of annotated chest x-ray data has been a major challenge in applying deep learning for medical image diagnoses ( 7 ). High fidelity ground truth labels require careful adjudication of images by clinical experts, which is time-consuming and tedious. Moreover, significant domain shifts between images generated from different scanners, different hospital sites, and different demographics makes it challenging to harness standard transfer learning techniques otherwise proven to be useful for real-world images in low-data regimes. Domain shifts are also noticeable between chest x-rays of critically ill patients, which are generally taken when the patient is in the supine position and can have several occluding objects such as electrodes, and non-critical patients. Recent efforts have been aimed towards curating large databases of labelled as well as unlabeled chest x-ray images ( 7 , 8 ) by leveraged the power of unstructured data, such as radiology notes ( 7 , 9 , 10 ). The primary methods explored are multi-modal data fusion, representation learning, or creating labels for datasets using a rule-based natural language processing (NLP) pipeline ( 11 ). Several studies have shown impressive performance in detecting and identifying chest x-ray abnormalities using CNNs and deep learning ( 12 , 13 ). Our work falls under the umbrella of rule-based NLP methods for extracting labels from unstructured radiology notes. In previous works, researchers extract positive (existence of clinically relevant CXR finding) or negative (absence of clinically relevant CXR finding) labels, while also including uncertainty labels to encode information from the notes which indicate possible, but not confirmed, existence of CXR findings ( 7 ). In this work, we hypothesize that labels derived from radiology notes are inherently noisy owing to variability in radiologists’ interpretations, language used, and differing documentation protocols. In clinical practice, discussions between consulting physicians and reading radiologist are not reflected in the labels. In addition, findings that are repetitive or beyond the indication, such as in the case for line placement confirmation often go unlabeled completely. In the case of labelling for bilateral opacities, the problem becomes more challenging owing to variability in the interpretation of the criterion. Added to this, radiologists may omit mentions of laterality, or might use non-standard language to indicate findings. Label noise can severely impact the true performance of deep learning models and for clinical decision-making tasks, this degradation in performance can come at a dire cost. Handling label noise, especially when extracting labels from other unstructured data sources, has received limited attention from the medical image analysis community ( 14 ). Here, we implement a knowledge distillation training paradigm that is trained on a small set of images with clean labels annotated by clinical experts, while leveraging a larger dataset of images with “pseudo-labels” or noisy labels derived from radiology notes. The clean ground truth labels for a subset of the larger dataset are obtained by annotations from clinical domain experts with inter-rater agreement. The main contributions of this work are as follows: A dataset of single-view chest radiograph images annotated by clinical experts for unilateral and bilateral pulmonary opacities, as well as equivocal images, with inter-rater agreement. A rule-based natural language processing pipeline to extract labels for bilateral opacities by named entity recognition and context understanding in radiology notes. A student-teacher training paradigm for learning from noisy labels, modelled on the knowledge distillation-based approach proposed by Li et. al ( 16 ) with results that show that our pipeline enables us to leverage unlabeled images to improve the classification performance of a convolutional neural network trained on a small subset of labeled data to identify bilateral pulmonary opacities on chest x-rays. Methodology: 1. Annotating Ground truth labels for bilateral opacities: This is a single-center retrospective cohort study at an academic institution. Included were patients diagnosed with Sepsis based on the Sepsis 3 criteria, admitted to the Intensive Care Unit at Emory University Hospital between August 2015 to May 2019. 8,000 single-view frontal chest radiographic images and their corresponding radiologist dictated reports were extracted from our database. 6000 of these images were annotated by three blinded physician experts for pulmonary opacities using the labels: ‘bilateral’, ‘left lung’, ‘right lung’, or ‘absent’. Images that were unclear or that could not be interpreted without certainty were labelled ‘Equivocal.’ Physician annotation was done by looking at single-view AP chest x-rays. 2. Extracting pseudo-labels for bilateral pulmonary opacities parsed from radiology notes: Radiology notes associated with each chest x-ray scan were parsed using a rule-based NLP pipeline to detect the presence of lung anomalies, following the method described by Irvin et al. ( 7 ). An NLP pipeline was designed using the medspaCy library, a framework for clinical text processing ( 15 ). The medspaCy pipeline comprised an in-built sentencizer for text preprocessing, target matcher for Named Entity Recognition, and a context module to detect the contexts of the named entities (such as negation, or possible likelihood). A sectionizer component was added to the pipeline as a pre-processing step to demarcate sections describing Clinical Indications, Impressions, and Findings in the Lungs/Pleura, Heart/Mediastinum, and Bones/Soft Tissue. Additional context and target rules derived from the language syntax frequently seen in our cohort of radiology notes were incorporated, as were phrases and context rules described by the Stanford CheXpert Labeler team ( 7 ). The following keywords were used to flag possible cases of pulmonary opacification: ARDS, atelectasis, consolidation, edema, effusion, opacity, infiltrates, pneumonia, and pneumothorax. Images were filters out based on mentions of these findings in associated radiology notes, then categorized as positive (indicating presence of finding), negative (indicating absence of finding) and uncertain (indicating possibility of existence requiring further clinical adjudication). Additionally, descriptors of the disease mentioned in the radiology notes were also detected, such as, ‘bilateral’ for ‘opacity’, ‘infiltrates’, and ‘pleural’ or ‘interstitial’ for ‘effusions’, or ‘edema’. To training a network to identify bilateral opacities, we used descriptors of laterality (‘Bilateral’, ‘Right’, ‘Left’) to generate pseudo-labels, labeling instances describing findings as only ‘Right’ or ‘Left’ as negative for bilateral opacities. Uncertainty labels were treated as positive for the purpose of this study. 3. Training the baseline CNN for detecting bilateral opacities on Chest Radiographs A CNN with Densenet121 architecture ( 16 ), followed by a fully connected layer, was trained to classify single channel chest radiograph images into three classes: ‘bilateral opacities present’, ‘bilateral opacities absent’, and ‘equivocal’. As a baseline, the network was trained on the entire cohort of 6000 ground-truth annotated images with a 70-30 patient level train-test split. The Adam optimizer ( 17 )was used with a learning rate of 1 × 10 -5 and the CNN was trained for 30 epochs. Focal loss ( 18 ) was used with gamma equal to 2 to facilitate model calibration. 4. Student-teacher training for leveraging a larger dataset with noisy labels: Motivated by the approach proposed by Li et. al. ( 19 ), a student-teacher training paradigm was implemented for learning from noisy labels with knowledge distillation. This method differs from standard knowledge distillation frameworks in that the teacher model is an auxiliary CNN model trained on a subset of a larger dataset, for which ground-truth annotations for the presence of bilateral pulmonary opacities are available. This is stage 1 of the training pipeline, and subsequently, the weights of teacher model are frozen. In the second stage of the training pipeline, the student model, which is an identical CNN model initialized with the weights of the teacher model, is trained on the entire training dataset with the noisy pseudo-labels derived from radiology notes. The weak supervision from such pseudo-labels is strengthened by incorporating guidance from the teacher model. A convex combination of the predicted output probabilities for each training image from the teacher model and the one-hot encoded pseudo-labels from the student model is used as the final target label to train the student network. Let f t * represent the optimal teacher neural network trained on image and “clean” label pairs ( x i , y i ), and let f s represent the student neural network classifier trained on image and pseudo-label pairs ( x i , s i ). If l represents the loss function used to train the teacher model, the loss used to train the student model, l s , is given by the following equation. l s ( f s ( x i ) , s i ) = l ( f s ( x i ) , λ s i + ( 1 − λ ) f t ( x i ) ) For our approach, l is the focal loss function with gamma equal to 2, and λ is chosen from a range of [0.3, 0.8] adaptively for varying levels of ground truth supervision using cross validation. Equivocal class predictions by the teacher model are retained as final predictions and these images are then filtered out of the training pipeline. The student model is trained for two output classes: ‘Bilateral Opacities Present’ and ‘Bilateral Opacities Absent’. The high-level training pipeline is presented in Figure 2 . Figure 2: Open in a new tab Student Teacher Training Framework for learning with noisy labels, applied to our problem statement of detecting bilateral opacities from chest x-ray images. 5. Model Evaluation: Out of the 6000 ground truth annotated images, 1350 are held out as a test set to evaluate our models (ensuring no patient-level data leakage). The rest are used to train the teacher models in stage 1 of training and are then augmented with 2000 pseudo-labelled images in stage 2 of training the student model. We first use all the remaining 4650 labelled images along with 2000 pseudo-labelled images resulting in a model trained with 60% ground truth supervision. We then reduce the number of ground truth labeled images used to train the teacher model, and pseudo-labels instead of their ground truth labels while training the student model as well, to study the effect of decreasing ground truth supervision on model performance. Evaluation metrics such as accuracy, precision, sensitivity, specificity, area under the receiver-operating characteristics curve (AUROC), area under the precision-recall curve (AUPRC), and f-score are reported in Table 1 . All results are reported as the mean and standard deviation after bootstrapping for 1000 iterations on the test set. Table 1. Performance metrics of student-teacher models trained on varying amounts of ground truth supervision. The highest values for each metric are emboldened. % Ground Truth Supervision Model Type Accuracy: mean (std) Precision: mean (std) Sensitivity: mean (std) Specificity: mean (std) F-score: mean (std) AUROC: mean (std) AUPRC: mean (std) 60 Teacher 0.839 (0.013) 0.813 (0.011) 0.952 (0.010) 0.686 (0.019) 0.874 (0.008) 0.879 (0.009) 0.887 (0.013) Student-Teacher 0.912 (0.010) 0.922 (0.009) 0.931 (0.009) 0.887 (0.013) 0.926 (0.007) 0.957 (0.005) 0.967 (0.005) 45 Teacher 0.778 (0.015) 0.777 (0.013) 0.876 (0.012) 0.637 (0.019) 0.823 (0.010) 0.845 (0.011) 0.867 (0.012) Student-Teacher 0.853 (0.010) 0.922 (0.009) 0.823 (0.013) 0.900 (0.012) 0.869 (0.009) 0.928 (0.007) 0.945 (0.007) 30 Teacher 0.775 (0.011) 0.793 (0.013) 0.839 (0.013) 0.684 (0.020) 0.815 (0.010) 0.824 (0.011) 0.856 (0.012) Student-Teacher 0.825 (0.010) 0.881 (0.011) 0.824 (0.013) 0.839 (0.016) 0.852 (0.009) 0.893 (0.009) 0.913 (0.010) 20 Teacher 0.741 (0.018) 0.729 (0.013) 0.896 (0.011) 0.555 (0.021) 0.804 (0.010) 0.786 (0.012) 0.806 (0.016) Student-Teacher 0.760 (0.013) 0.789 (0.014) 0.810 (0.014) 0.689 (0.011) 0.799 (0.011) 0.849 (0.010) 0.890 (0.009) Open in a new tab Results: The class-wise distribution of the labelled dataset was as follows: 65% of the dataset was labelled Bilateral Opacities Present, 23% of the dataset was labelled Bilateral Opacities Absent, and 12% of the dataset was labelled Equivocal. The results from the student-teacher models have been summarized in Table 1 . Four different student-teacher models were trained, using varying percentages of ground-truth supervision – 60%, 45%, 30% and 20%. The mean accuracy, precision, sensitivity, specificity, F-score, AUROC (Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristics), and AUPRC (Area Under the Precision-Recall Curve) along with the standard deviation values have been reported for all four models after bootstrapping on the test set. It can be noticed that training the student-teacher model with more data, albeit with noisy labels, drives up the accuracy, precision, AUROC and AUPRC at all levels of ground truth supervision. The student-teacher model trained with 60% ground truth supervision outperforms all models in terms of most metrics. An interesting observation is that the student-teacher model with 45% supervision performs almost on-par with that trained with 60% supervision in terms of precision, specificity and AUROC. It can also be seen that student models trained on a large training set with just 30% ground truth supervision (row 6 of Table 1 ) perform as well as a teacher model trained solely on all images with ground truth labels (row 1 of Table 1 ). Figure 3 shows the ROC (Receiving Operating Characteristic Curves) and PRC (Precision-Recall Curves) of the trained models, along with their confusion matrices. From the confusion matrices, the student models can better distinguish between the presence and absence of bilateral opacities (fraction of true negatives increases from 0.62 to 0.87), while the fraction of predictions for the equivocal class remains constant due to the design of our training pipeline. Figure 4 , 5 and 6 display Occlusion Sensitivity Maps and Grad-CAM ( 20 ) visualizations of the student model trained with 60% ground truth supervision, highlighting the image regions that were learned to be of high discriminatory importance for prediction of bilateral opacities. Figure 3: Open in a new tab Results for the positive class (Bilateral Opacities Present) of the student and teacher models with varying levels of ground truth supervision, bootstrapped 1000 times on the test set. Figure 4: Open in a new tab Occlusion Sensitivity Maps and Grad-CAM visualizations on test set cases of the ‘Equivocal’ class. The grad-CAM activations focus on regions of the lung that are occluded, or difficult to interpret with certainty due to poor quality. Figure 5: Open in a new tab Occlusion Sensitivity Maps and Grad-CAM visualizations on test set cases of the ‘Bilateral Opacities Present’ class. Here we can see that while the Grad-CAM either focusses on the entire lung region, or each lung separately, the occlusion sensitivity maps show us the exact regions of the image that were important when making the positive prediction. These regions correspond well with opacified lung spaces. Figure 6: Open in a new tab Occlusion Sensitivity Maps and Grad-CAM visualizations on test set cases of the ‘Bilateral Opacities Absent’ class. An interesting observation is that the Grad-CAM clearly pays attention to both lungs and correctly classifies the image as negative for bilateral opacities, despite one lung looking opacified. In figure 4 , we can notice via Grad-CAM image that the CNN pays attention to regions of the image that are occluded, of poor quality, or widely different from the norm, while classifying images as equivocal. In figure 5 , the occlusion sensitivity maps highlight exact regions of the input image that were important for classification of bilateral opacities. These correspond well with regions of lung opacification. Figure 6 clearly shows us how the CNN has learnt to distinguish between unilateral and bilateral opacities. The Grad-CAM visualization pays attention to both lung regions before confidently classifying the image as negative for bilateral opacities. Discussions: Recent research on Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) has moved towards early recognition as means of reducing severity and mortality, and initiation of therapies known to reduce the likelihood of ARDS development ( 21 ), like low tidal volume ventilation ( 22 ) and fluid restriction ( 23 ). Critically ill patients have frequent chest x-rays taken as a means of ensuring correct placement of devices such as chest tubes, central lines, or pacemaker, and for routine medical workups. Often times practice patterns for critically ill patients include frequent chest x-rays for various reasons, obtaining images multiple times in a 24 hour period. Using these images to help with accurate and early identification of bilateral pulmonary opacities can optimize patient care and ongoing clinical management. Similar works have been developed for early detection of ARDS, including the Lung Injury Prediction Score ( 24 , 25 ) which were derived from the coefficients of a Logistic Regression analysis, and machine learning tools utilizing eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) to predict the likelihood of ARDS using only EMR data ( 26 ). Other methods such as utilization of waveforms have been used to characterize autonomic dysfunctions, including heart rate variability (HRV) ( 27 – 29 ), however the influence of mechanical ventilation may introduce noise into the signal domain, reducing the utility of those approaches in isolation. Therefore, the utilization of imaging modalities, particularly with an emphasis on distinguishing quality, fidelity and robustness of chest x-rays may provide additional sources of information for machine learning models to correctly capture the saliences in the early phase of respiratory distress and the development of ARDS. In this work, we create a dataset of single-view chest x-ray images of critically ill patients annotated for bilateral opacities, unilateral opacities, or labelled equivocal, by three clinical experts with inter-rater agreement. We develop a deep learning model on our carefully annotated dataset that can learn to identify the presence of pulmonary opacities with an accuracy of 0.91 (95% CI 0.89, 0.92) and precision of 0.92 (95% CI 0.90, 0.93). High precision is an important metric for clinical alert systems as too many false positives can quickly fatigue clinicians on call. When developing Machine Learning solutions in clinical settings, trust and fairness is a significant concern ( 30 , 31 ). We address this in two ways. First, we train our model using focal loss, which has been proved to calibrate predicted output probabilities to how sure the model is of the classification ( 32 ). This makes it possible to threshold the output prediction to encode a level of confidence in our prediction. For example, we might threshold the predicted probability by 0.7 to ensure that alerts are raised only for images for which the model is 70% sure of the true class label. The second method of incorporating trust in our ML model is by labelling images that cannot be interpreted with certainty by clinicians as ‘equivocal’. These include images in which any region of the lung is occluded due to objects, poor quality, poor patient positioning etc. Instead of removing such images from our training and testing datasets, we explicitly force our CNN to learn to classify such images. This makes our model robust to such images that it will inevitably encounter in the clinical setting of managing critically ill patients. Figure 4 shows up promising results of the CNN identifying equivocal images. There are limitations to our work that we will now address. First, we only consider a subset of critically ill patients that were diagnosed with sepsis for the purpose of this study. To test the generalizability of our algorithm, it would be important to consider a broader cohort of patients, and possibly also perform an external validation study. Second, we propose our model as a tool for early detection of ARDS, however, we acknowledge that ARDS is a syndrome that can be diagnosed by identifying a group of symptoms. Thus, a multi-modal approach that utilizes EMR data, along with multiple CXR scans taken over the patient’s course of stay in the ICU might be more appropriate for improved diagnostic accuracy. Conclusion Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) is a serious lung injury with high mortality, that is often missed or diagnosed late, leading to poor patient outcomes. An important criterion for diagnosing ARDS is the presence of bilateral pulmonary opacification. In this work, we hypothesize an automated ARDS detection model which can generate predictive alerts for possible ARDS cases that could be applied in clinical and research domains. We create a unique dataset of single-view AP CXR images of critically ill patients labelled for bilateral opacities, unilateral opacities, and equivocal, by three clinical experts with inter-rater agreement. We augment this dataset with a larger dataset of CXR images and extract pseudo-labels for bilateral opacities using a rule-based NLP algorithm to parse associated radiology notes. We then train a DenseNet121 CNN model in a teacher-student framework designed to learn from a small subset of data with clean labels, and a larger set of data with noisy pseudo-labels. Our results show that we can achieve an accuracy of 91% (95% CI 89%, 92%), and AUC of 0.95 (95% CI 0.91-0.96) and a precision of 0.92 (95% CI 0.90, 0.93) in identifying bilateral opacities. Occlusion sensitivity maps and Grad-CAM visualizations show that the CNNs can extract intended information and identify opacities in one or both lungs when calculating the output prediction. Our model is robust to clinically equivocal images and provides reliable output probabilities calibrated to confidence in the presence of the bilateral pulmonary opacities. Acknowledgement: R.K. and M.A. were supported by the National Institutes of Health (NIH) Award Number R01GM139967 and UL1TR002378. C.M.D. was supported by NIH Award Number GM14’ and GM095442. Figures & Tables Figure 1: Open in a new tab Two examples to demonstrate Rule-based clinical keyword detection using our NLP pipeline. In the example on the left, the pipeline picks up on laterality labels. In the example on the right the pipeline effectively identifies uncertainty labels for findings from the Lung/Pleura sections. References: 1. Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome. JAMA. 2012 Jun 20;307(23) doi: 10.1001/jama.2012.6761. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Matthay MA, Thompson BT, Ware LB. The Berlin definition of acute respiratory distress syndrome: should patients receiving high-flow nasal oxygen be included? Lancet Respir Med. 2021 Aug;9(8):